<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold. Suppose <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth function, which is usually referred to as a <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian function by identifying <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}={\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}={\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the smooth function obtained by restricting <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t\}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t\}\times M</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Hamiltonian vector field <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{H_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector field on <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> determined by the formula</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(X_{H_{t}},\cdot)=dH_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(X_{H_{t}},\cdot)=dH_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A smooth map <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t):S^{1}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t):S^{1}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> solving the ordinary differential equation</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}(t)=X_{H_{t}}(x(t))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}(t)=X_{H_{t}}(x(t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is called a <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbit of <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}:M\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}:M\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the time <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> flow of <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{H_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then then the set of <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> has a one-to-one correspondence with the set of fixed points <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}:M\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}:M\to M</annotation></semantics></math> by evaluating <math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>. A periodic orbit <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is called nondegenerate if the linear map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\phi_{1}:T_{x(0)}M\to T_{x(0)}M" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\phi_{1}:T_{x(0)}M\to T_{x(0)}M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">does not have <math id="S1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> as an eigenvalue. If all <math id="S1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are nondegenerate, the Hamiltonian <math id="S1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is called nondegenerate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the symplectic manifold <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a contractible choice of almost complex structures on <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which are compatible with <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. The first Chern class of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(M,\omega)=c_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(M,\omega)=c_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined to be the first Chern class of <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TM</annotation></semantics></math> endowed with a choice of, equivalently, any choice of almost complex structure compatible with <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. The minimal Chern number of <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the nonnegative integer <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the range of the map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}(M)\xrightarrow{\text{Hurewicz}}H_{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{c_{1}(M)}\mathbb{Z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Hurewicz</mtext></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}(M)\xrightarrow{\text{Hurewicz}}H_{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{c_{1}(M)}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in{\mathbb{Z}}/2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in{\mathbb{Z}}/2N</annotation></semantics></math>, introduce the <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}}):=\bigoplus_{j\equiv i\text{ mod }2N}H_{j}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> mod </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}}):=\bigoplus_{j\equiv i\text{ mod }2N}H_{j}(M;{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Namely, we collapse the natural <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-grading on <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(M;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(M;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> to a <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2N</annotation></semantics></math>-grading. Because <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a finitely generated <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, there exist an integer <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence of integers <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}|a_{2}|\cdots|a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}|a_{2}|\cdots|a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}^{b_{i}}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}/a_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}/a_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})\cong{\mathbb{Z}}^{b_{i}}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}/a_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}/a_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the integers <math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are the invariant factors of <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a finitely generated <math id="S1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Using this decomposition, define the quantity</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}^{(2N)}(M):=\text{number of invariant factors of }H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>number of invariant factors of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}^{(2N)}(M):=\text{number of invariant factors of }H^{(2N)}_{i}(M;{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this paper is a solution to the homological Arnold conjecture over <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> for arbitrary closed symplectic manifolds. This bound is similar to the Morse inequality over <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> which takes into account the torsion part of the homology.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="Thmthma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold with minimal Chern number <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian. Then the number of <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded below by</span></p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{rank }H_{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})+2\sum_{i\in{\mathbb{Z}}/2N}\tau_{i}^{(2N)}(M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">rank </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{rank }H_{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})+2\sum_{i\in{\mathbb{Z}}/2N}\tau_{i}^{(2N)}(M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly comment on the historical background of the Arnold conjecture. Arnold conjectured (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arn78</a>, Appendix 9]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arn65</a>]</cite>) that the number of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of any nondegenerate Hamiltonian is at least the minimal number of critical points of a Morse function on <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strong</span> Arnold conjecture) and suggested that it is a consequence of a version of Morse inequality. While the strong Arnold conjecture is difficult to prove, there have been significant progresses towards the homological Arnold conjecture, namely, the number of periodic orbits being bounded from below by the total Betti number.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          From now on, in this paper, the Arnold conjecture refers to the homological version.</span></span></span> These progresses started with the breakthrough of Conley–Zehnder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">CZ83</a>]</cite>, who solved the strong Arnold conjecture for <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> using finite dimensional methods. Conley–Zehnder’s method was later extended by Floer to solve the strong Arnold conjecture for surfaces with genus at least <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and for certain classes of Kähler manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flo86</a>]</cite>. It was then followed by the revolutionary work of Floer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flo89</a>]</cite> which invented the Floer homology and established the Arnold conjecture for <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]=\lambda c_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]=\lambda c_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> (monotone symplectic manifolds). Floer’s result was extended by Hofer–Salamon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">HS95</a>]</cite> and Ono <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ono95</a>]</cite> to cover all semi-positive symplectic manifolds, i.e., <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which does not have <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\pi_{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\pi_{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(A)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(A)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3-n\leq c_{1}(A)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3-n\leq c_{1}(A)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. It is worth noting that these results hold over <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For general symplectic manifolds, Fukaya–Ono<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">FO99</a>]</cite>, Liu–Tian<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">LT98b</a>]</cite>, and Ruan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rua99</a>]</cite> proved the homological Arnold conjecture with lower bound coming from the <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rational</em> total Betti number. These papers are based on a kind of abstract machinery, generally called the “virtual technique,” which is designed for generalizing Floer’s construction (along side with the mathematical theory of Gromov–Witten invariants). The most recent advancement towards the homological Arnold conjecture by Abouzaid–Blumberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AB21</a>]</cite>, which relies more heavily on stable homotopy theory, bounds the number of periodic orbits from below by the sum of Betti numbers in any finite field. In addition to the aforementioned works, using different versions of the virtual technique, the weak Arnold conjecture over rational numbers is reproved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">FW22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> allows one to obtain a sharper lower bound for the number of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of a given non-degenerate Hamiltonian by treating the torsion components of <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(M;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(M;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly and simultaneously. The lower bound provided by <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm rank}H_{*}(M;\mathbb{F}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm rank}H_{*}(M;\mathbb{F}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> as from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AB21</a>]</cite> is also recovered from Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> by the universal coefficient theorem. Our bound is also strictly stronger than the bound of Abouzaid–Blumberg, for example, when <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\rm even}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>even</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\rm even}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> has only <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\rm odd}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>odd</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\rm odd}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> has only <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion and <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Proof strategy</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> is based on constructing a version of Hamiltonian Floer homology over the Novikov ring <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with integer coefficients and exponents, where</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda:=\Big\{\sum_{i=-m}^{\infty}a_{i}T^{i}\ |\ m\in{\mathbb{Z}},\ a_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}}\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda:=\Big\{\sum_{i=-m}^{\infty}a_{i}T^{i}\ |\ m\in{\mathbb{Z}},\ a_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}}\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and a comparison with the Morse homology of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients using the Piunikhin–Salamon–Schwarz (PSS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">PSS96</a>]</cite> map.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="Thmthma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed symplectic manifold such that <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the image of <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that the minimal Chern number of <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-degenerate <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian such that the symplectic action of any capped <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbit of <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> takes value in <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. After choosing an almost complex structure <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and some other auxiliary data, there is a <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2N</annotation></semantics></math>-graded chain complex</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">freely generated over <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by all contractible <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, graded by the Conley–Zehnder index, with differential given by suitably counting stable Floer trajectories <em id="Thmthma2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">with trivial isotropy group</em> connecting the <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span><span id="Thmthma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the same as in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Suppose <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth Morse function and let <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CM_{*}(f;\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CM_{*}(f;\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> be the Morse chain complex associated with <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the reduced <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2N</annotation></semantics></math>-grading. Then there exist a pair of <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-linear chain maps</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm PSS}:CM_{*}(f;\Lambda)\to CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm PSS}:CM_{*}(f;\Lambda)\to CF_{*}(H;\Lambda),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm SSP}:CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)\to CM_{*}(f;\Lambda)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm SSP}:CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)\to CM_{*}(f;\Lambda),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that their composition satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm SSP}\circ\Psi^{\rm PSS}=Id+\text{terms with positive $T$-exponent}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">terms with positive </mtext><mi>T</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-exponent</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm SSP}\circ\Psi^{\rm PSS}=Id+\text{terms with positive $T$-exponent}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the assumptions in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, Theorem <a href="#Thmthma3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> implies that the induced map on homology</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm PSS}:H_{*}(M;\Lambda)\to HF_{*}(H;\Lambda)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm PSS}:H_{*}(M;\Lambda)\to HF_{*}(H;\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">in an injection. Using the algebraic arguments in Section <a href="#S4.SS7" title="4.7. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, we see that Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> holds in this setting.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is easy to see that if <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the image of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})\to H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and the symplectic action of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are all <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued, Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> also holds. Indeed, one can suitably rescale the symplectic form <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by a positive integer to reduce to the integral setting, because such a rescaling process does not change the number of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">In general, we can choose a sequence of symplectic forms <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> which represent rational cohomology classes and converge to <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate, then for <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large, the <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has a one-to-one correspondence with the <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, without loss of generality, we can assume that <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> represents a rational cohomology class. Based on the elementary discussion after Hypothesis <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Hypothesis 4.1. ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we can modify <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian whose <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits all have <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued symplectic action without changing the number of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits. Thus the theorem follows from the previous discussions.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> has two major steps, which account for the most important novelties of this paper. First, we show that any moduli space of stable Floer trajectories can be <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">globally</em> presented as the zero locus of a continuous section on a <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smooth normally complex</em> orbifold vector bundle over a <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smooth normally complex</em> orbifold, by generalizing a recent result of Abouzaid–McLean–Smith <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMS21</a>]</cite> which works for the moduli space of closed genus <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves. Moreover, we show that these presentations are coherent, packaged using the language of <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flow categories</em> introduced by Cohen–Jones–Segal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CJS95</a>]</cite>. Second, we apply the <em id="S1.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fukaya–Ono–Parker (FOP) perturbation scheme</em> introduced in our early work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite>, which was originally proposed by Fukaya–Ono <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">FO97</a>]</cite>, to obtain <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued virtual fundamental chains from the aforementioned presentations of the moduli spaces of stable Floer trajectories, and show that the induced algebraic counts from moduli spaces of virtual dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> can be organized to define a chain complex over <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> freely generated by contractible <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> is quite similar to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, except that the relevant moduli spaces parametrize solutions of the PSS/SSP equations, which are also known as the “spiked discs.” We package these moduli spaces and their presentations using the language of <em id="S1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flow bimodules</em>, which are investigated systematically in the symplectic context in the recent work of Abouzaid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abo22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we give a slightly more detailed description of the technical ingredients.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Flow categories and derived orbifold presentation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we discuss about the geometric construction of compatible global charts for the moduli spaces arising from the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a closed symplectic manifold <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and a nondegenerate <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CJS95</a>]</cite>, one can introduce a topologically enriched category <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{{\rm Floer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{{\rm Floer}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objects are capped <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The morphism space from <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the compact Hausdorff topological space <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space of stable Floer trajectories connecting <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,r,q\in T^{{\rm Floer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,r,q\in T^{{\rm Floer}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the composition map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{rq}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{rq}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is given by the concatenation which forms a broken Floer trajectory breaking at <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. The composition maps are homeomorphisms onto their images and satisfy the natural associativity relation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The composition maps naturally equip the space <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> with a stratification structure, with strata indexed by words of the form <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q</annotation></semantics></math>-stratum of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is homeomorphic to the product</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{r_{l}q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{r_{l}q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The moduli space <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is only an orbispace in general, though the usual Kuranishi reduction process describes it <em id="S1.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally</em> as the zero locus of a section on an orbifold vector bundle over an orbifold. Moreover, even if every <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> has a global Kuranishi model, its restriction to the boundary stratum <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math> may not be the product of the global Kuranishi models on each factor. This would obstruct any meaningful inductive construction on <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{{\rm Floer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{{\rm Floer}}</annotation></semantics></math> from these Kuranishi models, leaving alone the issue concerning the <em id="S1.SS2.p2.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothness</em> of the (thickened) moduli spaces. We solve all of these problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(See Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 4.7. ‣ 4.5. Main Theorems ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>)
Let <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:S^{1}\times M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Then for any capped <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic orbits <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist a smooth effective normally complex orbifold with corners <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same stratification structure as <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, a smooth normally complex orbifold vector bundle <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, a continuous section <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}:{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}:{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi_{pq}:{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{pq}:{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which defines an isomorphism of orbispaces.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We call the quadruple <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq},{\mathcal{S}}_{pq},\psi_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq},{\mathcal{S}}_{pq},\psi_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> a <em id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">derived orbifold chart</em> (D-chart for short) of <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given an index <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q</annotation></semantics></math>, one can take the product</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{U}}_{r_{l}q},{\mathcal{E}}_{pr_{1}}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{U}}_{r_{l}q},{\mathcal{S}}_{pr_{1}}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{r_{l}q},\psi_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\psi_{r_{l}q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⊞</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⊞</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{U}}_{r_{l}q},{\mathcal{E}}_{pr_{1}}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{U}}_{r_{l}q},{\mathcal{S}}_{pr_{1}}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{r_{l}q},\psi_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\psi_{r_{l}q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which defines a D-chart of <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(See Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 4.7. ‣ 4.5. Main Theorems ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>)
The restriction of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq},{\mathcal{S}}_{pq},\psi_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq},{\mathcal{S}}_{pq},\psi_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> to the stratum of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the stabilization (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Definition 2.3. ‣ 2.1.1. Derived orbifold charts ‣ 2.1. Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on 𝒵_𝑑 ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) of the product chart (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.2. Flow categories and derived orbifold presentation ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) by a complex orbifold vector bundle.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If the above data satisfy some further compatibility conditions spelled out in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm24" title="Definition 3.24. ‣ 3.4.4. Derived orbifold lifts of flow categories and bimodules ‣ 3.4. Derived orbifold charts with stratifications ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a>, we call this system of derived orbifold charts of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> a (normally complex) <em id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">derived orbifold lift</em> of the flow category <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{{\rm Floer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{{\rm Floer}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The full statement of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 4.7. ‣ 4.5. Main Theorems ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> exactly asserts the existence of an oriented and normally complex derived orbifold lift of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{{\rm Floer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{{\rm Floer}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned above, the construction of derived orbifold chart for a single moduli space <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Floer}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is based on generalizing a recent result of Abouzaid–McLean–Smith <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMS21</a>, Theorem 1.7]</cite>. We replace their framed <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic spheres by framed <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic cylinders and modify the perturbation method accordingly. The main difficulty is the compatibility mentioned above. To this end, we introduce the <em id="S1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-layered thickening</em> (see Section <a href="#S5.SS3.SSS3" title="5.3.3. Multi-layered thickening ‣ 5.3. Global chart construction II ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3.3</span></a>) to make sure that the thickened moduli space <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{U}}_{r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{U}}_{r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math> is truly embedded in <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. To endow the space <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> with a smooth structure, we follow the application of classical smoothing theory from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMS21</a>]</cite>. However, because <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orbifold with corners in general, the traditional smoothing theory does not suffice for our purpose. We develop a <em id="S1.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative equivariant smoothing</em> technique to construct the smoothing in an inductive fashion, which also makes the resulting smooth structures on various moduli spaces compatible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We expect that the construction of derived orbifold charts presented in this paper could be useful in the study of symplectic field theory (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGH00</a>]</cite> because the methods presented can be used to deal with punctures.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The derived orbifold chart lift of the flow category <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math> should also be applied to construct certain Hamiltonian Floer homotopy theory for a complex-oriented generalized cohomology theory. As a first application, these geometric constructions should simplify the definition of Floer Morava <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AB21</a>]</cite> because the algebraic gluing of local virtual fundamental chains should be largely simplified given these global charts.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to remark that there are some other technical issues one needs to resolve in order to carry out our perturbation scheme on the derived orbifold charts. For instance, to make sure an inductive scheme could work, one needs to guarantee that any prescribed construction on the “boundary statum” (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.2. Flow categories and derived orbifold presentation ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) can be extended to a neighborhood of it in <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math>. To this end, a suitable collar structure is necessary and one has to keep track of the compatibility of such collar structures with the “difference bundle” appearing in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.2. Flow categories and derived orbifold presentation ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. Considerations of this form partially account for the length of the later sections, because we will need a more refined geometric construction compared to the one in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMS21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Morse function <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a Riemannian metric <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, if the pair <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies certain properties stronger than the usual Morse–Smale condition, the work of Wehrheim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weh12</a>]</cite> shows that the moduli spaces of unpamatrized gradient flow lines of <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> are compact smooth manifolds with corners. Therefore, one can associate <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> with a flow category <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Morse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Morse}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objects are “capped” critical points of <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, see Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Morse flow category ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The morphism space <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{xy}^{{\rm Morse}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Morse</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{xy}^{{\rm Morse}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the moduli space of unparametrized gradient flow lines connecting the underlying critical points of <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> if the cappings are the same, otherwise it is the empty set.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The composition</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{xz}^{{\rm Morse}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{zy}^{{\rm Morse}}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{xy}^{{\rm Morse}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Morse</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Morse</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Morse</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{xz}^{{\rm Morse}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{zy}^{{\rm Morse}}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{xy}^{{\rm Morse}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is defined to be the concatenation of gradient flow lines, which is a diffeomorphism onto its image.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To relate the two flow categories <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Morse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Morse}</annotation></semantics></math>, we introduce the PSS and SSP <em id="S1.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flow bimodules</em> <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> is described by the following data:</p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in T^{\rm Morse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in T^{\rm Morse}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>, we associate the pair <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,p)</annotation></semantics></math> with the moduli space of solutions to PSS equations <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{xp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>PSS</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{xp}</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The flow category <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Morse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Morse}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> on the left, i.e., there is a map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Morse}_{xy}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{yp}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{xp}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Morse</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>PSS</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>PSS</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm Morse}_{xy}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{yp}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{xp}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying an associativity condition. Geometrically, this map is again given by concatenating solutions to gradient flow line equations and Floer-type equations, and it is a homeomorphism onto its image.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The flow category <math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> on the right in a similar fashion, i.e., there is a map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm PSS}_{xq}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm Floer}}_{qp}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{xp}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>PSS</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>PSS</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{\rm PSS}_{xq}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm Floer}}_{qp}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}^{{\rm PSS}}_{xp}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying an associativity condition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The right and left actions should satisfy certain associativity relation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The flow bimodule <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math> is characterized similarly. Just like flow categories, one can define the notion of derived orbifold lift for flow bimodules (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm25" title="Definition 3.25. ‣ 3.4.4. Derived orbifold lifts of flow categories and bimodules ‣ 3.4. Derived orbifold charts with stratifications ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.25</span></a>). It turns out that we can indeed construct a derived orbifold lift for both <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math> via geometric arguments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(See Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8. ‣ 4.5. Main Theorems ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>)
Each of the flow bimodules <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a derived orbifold lift (which extends the D-chart lift of <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math> and the trivial lift of <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Morse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Morse}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to these statements, the geometric underpinning of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> also needs certain moduli spaces designed for interpolating between <math id="S1.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm SSP}\circ\Psi^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm SSP}\circ\Psi^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math> and the identity map on <math id="S1.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(M;\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(M;\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a structure should be better thought of as a morphism between two flow bimodules “parametrized” by the interval <math id="S1.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, as exploited in detail in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abo22</a>]</cite>. We do not follow this viewpoint in this paper. Instead, we simply list out the necessary geometric input in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Fukaya–Ono–Parker perturbations</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we discuss about the perturbation methods adapted in the course of the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbation scheme to be used in our construction was proposed by Fukaya–Ono <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">FO97</a>]</cite> and further developed by B. Parker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par13</a>]</cite>. So this scheme will be referred to as the FOP perturbation scheme. We give a detailed introduction to its idea. For complete details, the reader could consult <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite>. In a moduli problem related to pseudo-holomorphic curves, objects (such as stable maps) may have nontrivial automorphism groups; in the case of Hamiltonian Floer theory, the sphere bubbles in a stable Floer trajectory may be multiple covers carrying nontrivial automorphisms. This means that the moduli spaces have certain orbifold-type behavior. It is well-known that transversality could not always be achieved in the orbifold setting. To define invariants using abstract perturbations (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">FO99</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">LT98a</a>]</cite>), one needs to use “multi-valued perturbations” and count of transverse zeroes with <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued weights. Such constructions result in <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued invariants or chain complexes with <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients. Another more algebraic reasoning to explain the appearance of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is that Poincaré duality for oriented orbifolds only holds over <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see the discussions of Pardon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>]</cite>). These two perspectives could account for the reasons why Gromov–Witten invariants and Hamiltonian Floer homology of a general symplectic manifold are only defined over the field of rational numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us set up a finite-dimensional model to illustrate the failure of equivariant transversality and the proposal of Fukaya–Ono. Consider a compact smooth manifold <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> acted on by a finite group <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a representation of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We may regard the orbifold <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the moduli space and the orbifold vector bundle <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U\times{\bm{W}})/{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U\times{\bm{W}})/{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the obstruction bundle. To simplify the exposition even further, let us assume <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm dim}U={\rm dim}{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm dim}U={\rm dim}{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, a weighted count of the zeroes of an equivariant transverse maps <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:U\to{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:U\to{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> will give an Euler number, which is an invariant of the obstruction bundle over the orbifold <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the equivariance of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> may contain higher dimensional pieces; for example, when <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains no trivial subreprsentations, the fixed point locus <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> must be contained in <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence ordinary transversality cannot be achieved equivariantly in general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The existence of a <em id="S1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normal complex structure</em> allows one to consider a more delicate kind of section so that one form of equivariant transversality <em id="S1.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">can</em> hold. In the above finite-dimensional model, consider the normal bundle <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed point set <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, consider the decomposition</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}=\mathring{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\oplus\check{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}=\mathring{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\oplus\check{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of trivial subrepresentations and <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of nontrivial irreducible subrepresentations. A normal complex structure in this case is a <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant complex structure on both <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To illustrate the FOP perturbation scheme, assume for simplicity that <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial with fiber a complex <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}=\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\bm{W}}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}=\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A section <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> defined near <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be regarded as a map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to C^{\infty}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})^{\mathit{\Gamma}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to C^{\infty}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})^{\mathit{\Gamma}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the space of smooth <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant maps from <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">FO97</a>]</cite> Fukaya–Ono proposed to consider sections corresponding to fiberwise polynomial maps</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">i.e., sections of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to each normal fiber is an equivariant complex polynomial map of degree at most <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\times{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\times{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote the corresponding section by <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{f}:U\to{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{f}:U\to{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one has</p>
<table id="S1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{f}^{-1}(0)=U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\cup\big(S_{f}^{-1}(0)\cap(U\setminus U^{\mathit{\Gamma}})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{f}^{-1}(0)=U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\cup\big(S_{f}^{-1}(0)\cap(U\setminus U^{\mathit{\Gamma}})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Although the zero locus of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> still contains <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Fukaya–Ono asserted that the count of the second component (modulo <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>), is an invariant, if <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large and <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is generic. If this is true, one could indeed define an integer-valued “Euler number” by counting the zeroes of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with trivial stabilizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A difficulty to implement Fukaya–Ono’s idea is that we need to introduce a new notion of transversality for those fiberwise polynomial maps. Certain delicate properties are needed for this transversality notion, especially when we change the cut-off degree <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for polynomial maps and when we change the isotropy groups. The crucial idea in the preprint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par13</a>]</cite> of Brett Parker which addresses these difficulties played an important role in the recent construction of the authors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite>. In short, one can define a canonical notion of transversality for those normally polynomial perturbations which behaves well when we change the degree <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the isotropy group and which is satisfifed by generic such perturbations such that the integral counting is well-defined as proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">FO97</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Going back to the Floer-theoretic construction, given a derived orbifold lift of the flow category <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to construct the FOP perturbation on all the thickened moduli spaces inductively. Although such a construction is cumbersome and somewhat routine, one novelty showing up is the <em id="S1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiplicativity</em> of the FOP perturbation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(See Corollary 2.8)
Let <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> be normally complex derived orbifold charts (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Definition 2.1. ‣ 2.1.1. Derived orbifold charts ‣ 2.1. Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on 𝒵_𝑑 ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) such that <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongly transverse FOP section (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Definition 2.7. ‣ 2.1.3. FOP sections and strong transversality ‣ 2.1. Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on 𝒵_𝑑 ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>). Then the product section</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}:{\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}:{\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is also a strongly transverse FOP section.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The upshot of the inductive construction of FOP perturbations can be summarized schematically as follows, after defining the relevant counts using <math id="S1.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional moduli spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(See Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm49" title="Theorem 3.49. ‣ 3.7. Constructing FOP perturbations ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.49</span></a>)
Using the derived orbifold lift of <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can define the chain complex <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H;\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> after choosing a compatible family of strongly transverse FOP perturbations. Similarly, the chain maps <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the homotopy</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\rm SSP}\circ\Psi^{\rm PSS}=Id+O(T)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\rm SSP}\circ\Psi^{\rm PSS}=Id+O(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">can be constructed by choosing a compatible family of strongly transverse FOP perturbations on the derived orbifold lifts of the relevant moduli spaces.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As a consequence, Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmthma3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Proof strategy ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> hold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to point out that the definition of FOP sections depend on an extra structure which is called a <em id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">straightening</em> (see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm31" title="Definition 3.31 (Straightenings). ‣ 3.5.3. Straightening ‣ 3.5. Additional structures ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.31</span></a>). Heuristically, it consists of a suitable Riemannian metric on <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and a special form of connection on <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> for each of the derived orbifold charts appearing in the derived orbifold lift. Again, there is an issue of compatibility with various structures on our derived orbifold lift. Although the relevant compatibility conditions will be shown to hold after a somewhat routine and cumbersome induction construction, pinning down the correct formulations seems to be a nontrivial task.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4. </span>Discussions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to comment on the implications of our result and some notable features of the techniques.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.1. </span>Towards stronger forms of the Arnold conjecture</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One important quantity in the statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> is the minimal Chern number. In the extremal case when <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math>, if the only torsion components of <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/3</annotation></semantics></math>, and both of them have odd degree, then they together contribute <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to the quantity (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>). In other words, our lower bound is only as strong as the lower bound from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AB21</a>]</cite> in this special case. This might suggest that the sharp lower bound in the Arnold conjecture might need to incorporate certain symplectic information from the ambient symplectic manifold, as witnessed by the minimal Chern number in our statement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, it is possible that the methods developed in this paper might eventually resolve the <em id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong</em> Arnold conjecture for simply connected closed symplectic manifolds of dimension <math id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, following the bifurcation methods initiated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flo88</a>]</cite>, it should be possible to study the simple homotopy type of our integral Floer chain complex, which in turn is closely related to the <em id="S1.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable</em> Morse number of the ambient manifold. When the ambient manifold satisfies the condition at the beginning of this paragraph, the stable Morse number actually coincides with the Morse number.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.2. </span>Stable/normal complex structure</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As emphasized in the exposition of FOP perturbations, the normal complex structure of derived orbifold charts plays a crucial role even in the definition of these polynomial-like perturbations. The closely related notion of stable complex structures also plays an important role in the construction of Floer homotopy type in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AB21</a>]</cite>, in which the stable complex structures are necessary for applying Poibcaré duality to the Morava K-theory of the classifying space of orbifolds. These structures are not necessary for the proof of Arnold conjecture over <math id="S1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is natural to expect more applications which make essential use of the normal/stable complex structures on the moduli spaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.4.3. </span>Algebraic structures and operations</h4>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the concept of “flow multimodules” as developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abo22</a>]</cite>, one should be able to define multiplicative structure on the integral Floer homology. For instance, it should be possible to generalize the definition of quantum Steenrod operations on Hamiltonian Floer theory of semi-positive symplectic manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil20</a>]</cite> to all symplectic manifolds using the <math id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-reduction of our Hamiltonian Floer homology. Our construction of derived orbifold lifts should also be useful for regularizing moduli spaces of <math id="S1.SS4.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curves originated from the algebraic structures.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.5. </span>Outline</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following describes the content of every section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we review the differential topology related to the perturbation scheme used in this paper. Most notably, the multiplicativity of the FOP strongly transverse condition is derived in Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm14" title="Corollary 2.14. ‣ 2.2.1. Products of transverse FOP sections ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is devoted to the discussions of flow categories, flow bimodules and their derived orbifold lifts. We show that for a flow category whose derived orbifold lift has a normal complex structure, it is possible to construct a family of compatible strongly transverse FOP perturbations so that one can define a chain complex over <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> from these data if there is a further compatible orientation structure. The discussions culminate at Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm49" title="Theorem 3.49. ‣ 3.7. Constructing FOP perturbations ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.49</span></a>. A similar result for flow bimodules is presented as well.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We bring back concrete symplectic geometry in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. In particular, we describe the flow categories <math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Morse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Morse}</annotation></semantics></math>, and flow bimodules <math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the structures of the interpolating moduli spaces in detail. After presenting the main geometric statements in Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Main Theorems ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> and the necessary input from a <math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter family of PSS/SSP moduli spaces (“the chain homotopy moduli spaces”) in Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6. The homotopy ‣ 4. Proof of the integral Arnold conjecture ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, we prove our main theorem.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we show how to construct a compatible family of <em id="S1.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">topological</em> global Kuranishi charts for the moduli spaces <math id="S1.I4.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Smoothing ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, We discuss how to use (relative) equivariant smoothing theory to endow the global Kuranishi charts constructed in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> with smooth structures. Moreover, we describe how to construct a normally complex lift of all derived orbifold lifts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The parallel constructions for PSS, SSP type moduli spaces as in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Smoothing ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> are presented in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Constructions for PSS, SSP, and the homotopy ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Because most of the arguments are only a matter of cosmetic modification, most of the proofs are only sketched.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We were informed by Semon Rezchikov on September 14, 2022 that he had an independent approach towards similar results following our early work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank Mohammed Abouzaid, Kenji Fukaya, Helmut Hofer, Suguru Ishikawa, Alexander Kupers, John Pardon, Mohan Swaminathan, and Weiwei Wu for interesting discussions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is completed during the second author’s visit at the Institute for Advanced Study hosted by Professor Helmut Hofer. He would like to thank IAS and Professor Hofer for their hospitality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second author would like to express his sincere gratitude to his family, especially his wife, Dr. Ning Lin, for her understanding, support, advice, encouragement, and love.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we briefly review the construction of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite> concerning the Fukaya–Ono–Parker perturbations and prove an additional property of FOP perturbations regarding the multiplicativity. Some necessary ingredients from the theory of Whitney stratifications are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we describe the setup for FOP perturbations. In this section we consider effective<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            A stabilization can make a non-effective orbifold effective.</span></span></span> orbifolds only. An <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">orbifold</span> is a locally compact, Hausdorff, and second countable topological space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with an atlas of orbifold charts: each chart is of the form <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(U,{\mathit{\Gamma}},\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(U,{\mathit{\Gamma}},\psi)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite group with an effective linear action on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an invariant open subset, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:U\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:U\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant continuous map such that the induced map <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homeomorphism onto an open subset of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a compatibility requirement for overlapping charts which we will not recall here. In notation the map <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is often suppressed and we identify its image with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U/{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, over an orbifold <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, an orbifold vector bundle consists of an orbifold <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a continuous map <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{E}}:{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{E}}:{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an atlas of bundle charts of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A bundle chart consists of a chart <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(U,{\mathit{\Gamma}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(U,{\mathit{\Gamma}})</annotation></semantics></math> of the base <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant vector bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:E\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:E\to U</annotation></semantics></math>, and a homeomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E/{\mathit{\Gamma}}\cong\pi_{\mathcal{E}}^{-1}(U/{\mathit{\Gamma}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E/{\mathit{\Gamma}}\cong\pi_{\mathcal{E}}^{-1}(U/{\mathit{\Gamma}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with the projections. We denote by the triple <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)</annotation></semantics></math> a bundle chart. We ofter use charts which are centered at certain points of the orbifold. For each point <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, an orbifold chart <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">centered at</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a chart <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{x}=(U_{x},{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},\psi_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{x}=(U_{x},{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},\psi_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an invariant open neighborhood of the origin, such that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{x}(0)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{x}(0)=x</annotation></semantics></math>. A bundle chart for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is then denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},E_{x},U_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},E_{x},U_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},U_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},U_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is an orbifold chart centered at <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{x}\to U_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{x}\to U_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant vector bundle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We need a few other frequently used notations. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)</annotation></semantics></math> be a bundle chart. For each subgroup <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G}\subset U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G}\subset U</annotation></semantics></math> the fixed point locus of the induced <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action and denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> the normal bundle of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G}\hookrightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G}\hookrightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the fibers of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are representations of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> whose decompositions into the direct sum of irreducible representations contain no trivial summand. We can also decompose the restriction</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E|_{U^{G}}=\mathring{E}^{G}\oplus\check{E}^{G}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E|_{U^{G}}=\mathring{E}^{G}\oplus\check{E}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{E}^{G}\subset E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{E}^{G}\subset E</annotation></semantics></math> is the subbundle whose fibers are the maximal trivial subrepresentations and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{E}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{E}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement, whose fibers are direct sums of nontrivial irreducible representations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1. </span>Derived orbifold charts</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the notion of derived orbifold charts introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par21</a>, Section 5]</cite> and used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite>, as well as a few related concepts. We will soon generalize this notion to the case of orbifolds with faces but this generalization is straightforward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An (effective) <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">derived orbifold chart</span> (<span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D-chart</span> for short) is a triple <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective orbifold, <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orbifold vector bundle, and <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous section. We say the triple <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> is compact if <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is compact.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A (smooth) <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">perturbation</span> of a compact derived orbifold chart <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth section <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a precompact open neighborhood <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> and a continuous norm on <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}\|_{C^{0}({\mathcal{U}}\setminus{\mathcal{D}})}&lt;\inf_{x\in{\mathcal{U}}\setminus{\mathcal{D}}}|{\mathcal{S}}(x)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}\|_{C^{0}({\mathcal{U}}\setminus{\mathcal{D}})}&lt;\inf_{x\in{\mathcal{U}}\setminus{\mathcal{D}}}|{\mathcal{S}}(x)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is still contained in <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is also a compact derived orbifold chart.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normal complex structure</span> on a derived orbifold chart <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of, for each bundle chart <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)</annotation></semantics></math> and for each subgroup <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subseteq{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subseteq{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant complex structure <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant complex structure <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{E}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{E}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, these complex structures are compatible in the following sense.</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within the same chart, for each pair of subgroups <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> for which we have <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant inclusions</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ NU^{H}|_{U^{G}}\subset NU^{G},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>H</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ NU^{H}|_{U^{G}}\subset NU^{G},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \check{E}^{H}|_{U^{G}}\subset\check{E}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \check{E}^{H}|_{U^{G}}\subset\check{E}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we require that they are complex linear with respect to the complex structures <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{H},J_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{H},J_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The system of invariant complex structures are compatible with chart embeddings.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">straightening</span> of a derived orbifold chart <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a Riemannian metric on <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a connection on <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following conditions.</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each chart <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U,E,{\mathit{\Gamma}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U,E,{\mathit{\Gamma}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the pullback Riemannian metric <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is “straightened.” Namely, for each subgroup <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, near <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, the ambient Riemannian metric <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the bundle metric on <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> via the exponential map along the normal directions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each chart <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U,E,{\mathit{\Gamma}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U,E,{\mathit{\Gamma}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the pullback connection <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∇</mo><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is “straightened.” Namely, for each subgroup <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can identify a neighborhood of <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with a neighborhood of the zero section of <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> using the exponential map associated with <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. After identifying <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E|_{NU^{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E|_{NU^{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the pullback of <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E|_{U^{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E|_{U^{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the projection <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{G}\to U^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{G}\to U^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and the parallel transport along normal geodesics using <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∇</mo><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, the connection <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∇</mo><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the pullback connection of the restriction of <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∇</mo><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I1.i4.I1.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Lemma 3.15, Lemma 3.20]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For a compact derived orbifold chart <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a straightening in a neighborhood of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are several natural relations between derived orbifold charts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">open embedding</span> from a derived orbifold chart <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of an open embedding <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of orbifolds and a bundle isomorphism <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\phi}:{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime}|_{\phi({\mathcal{U}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\phi}:{\mathcal{E}}\to{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime}|_{\phi({\mathcal{U}})}</annotation></semantics></math> covering <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\phi}\circ{\mathcal{S}}={\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}\circ\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\phi}\circ{\mathcal{S}}={\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}\circ\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\subset\phi({\mathcal{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\subset\phi({\mathcal{U}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">germ</span> of open embeddings from a derived orbifold chart <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalence class of open embeddings from an open neighborhood of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> with the restrictions of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> over it, to <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the equivalence relation is induced by shrinking the neighborhood. Such a germ is called to induce a <span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">germ equivalence</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">product</span> of derived orbifold charts <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, is the derived orbifold chart</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{U}}_{k},{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{k},{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{U}}_{k},{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{k},{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus\cdots\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}=({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}=({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a derived orbifold chart and <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> be another orbifold vector bundle. The <span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stabilization</span> of <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the chart</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Stab}_{\mathcal{F}}({\mathcal{C}})=({\mathcal{F}},\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\oplus\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}},\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{S}}\oplus\tau_{\mathcal{F}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Stab}_{\mathcal{F}}({\mathcal{C}})=({\mathcal{F}},\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\oplus\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}},\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{S}}\oplus\tau_{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological section.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The stabilization operation intertwines with many other constructions. For example, suppose <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:{\mathcal{C}}\to{\mathcal{C}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:{\mathcal{C}}\to{\mathcal{C}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open embedding and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a orbifold vector bundle. Denote by <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> the pullback bundle <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is an obvious extension of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> which defines an open embedding from <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Stab}_{\mathcal{F}}({\mathcal{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Stab}_{\mathcal{F}}({\mathcal{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Stab}_{{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime}}({\mathcal{C}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Stab}_{{\mathcal{F}}^{\prime}}({\mathcal{C}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, if <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with a straightening, one can endow the stabilization <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Stab}_{\mathcal{F}}({\mathcal{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Stab}_{\mathcal{F}}({\mathcal{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> with a straightening naturally once <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with a bundle metric and a compatible connection.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2. </span>Equivariant polynomial maps</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To describe the use of FOP sections we need to recall some basic properties of equivariant polynomial maps. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite group and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> be finite dimensional complex <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-representations. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of equivariant complex polynomial maps from <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each nonnegative integer <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\subset{\rm Poly}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\subset{\rm Poly}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the subspace of maps with degree at most <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a natural <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant evaluation map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}:{\bm{V}}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\to{\bm{W}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}:{\bm{V}}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\to{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whose zero locus is denoted by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=Z_{d}^{G}:=Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=Z_{d}^{G}:=Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This is a complex algebraic variety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The application of FOP sections crucially relies on the existence of certain Whitney stratifications on the above variety. By a classical theorem of Whitney <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Whi65</a>]</cite>, any complex algebraic subvariety inside a smooth variety admits a canonical Whitney stratification (see Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem A.4. ‣ A.1. Basics of Whitney stratifications ‣ Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>) whose strata are smooth algebraic submanifolds. However, for the variety <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1.2. Equivariant polynomial maps ‣ 2.1. Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on 𝒵_𝑑 ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), this canonical Whitney stratification may not respect the group action. One needs to use a “more symmetric” Whitney stratification with a few nice properties—this was the observation of B. Parker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par13</a>]</cite>. The necessity of having such nice properties comes from the consideration that we need to consider the Whitney stratifications on the variety <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> for different cut-off degrees <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and different groups <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. In our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Theorem 4.3]</cite> we proved the following result showing the existence of certain canonical “symmetric” Whitney stratifications which have the desired nice properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a unique Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each subgroup </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, let </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\subset{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\subset{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be the subset of points whose stabilizer is exactly </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then for each </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\cap({\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\cap({\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the germ through </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is contained in </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Whitney stratification is the minimal one among all which satisfy the above condition.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, this Whitney stratification enjoys the following additional properties.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is </span><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-invariant.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is induced from a Whitney prestratification (see Definition </span><a href="#A1.Thmthm1" title="Definition A.1. ‣ A.1. Basics of Whitney stratifications ‣ Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><a href="#A1.Thmthm2" title="Definition A.2 (Whitney stratification). ‣ A.1. Basics of Whitney stratifications ‣ Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) on </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> whose strata are all algebraic submanifolds.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is invariant under all </span><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant diffeomorphisms of </span><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which preserve </span><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The nice behaviors of the canonical Whitney stratification allow us to extend our consideration to bundles. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth manifold acted on trivially by a finite group <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V,W\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V,W\to B</annotation></semantics></math> be smooth <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant complex vector bundles with fibers isomorphic to representations <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Such datum defines a locally trivial bundle</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)\to B" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)\to B</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">whose fiber at <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in B</annotation></semantics></math> is the space <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a subbundle</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)\subset V\oplus{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)\subset V\oplus{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">whose fiber at <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the zero locus associated with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}:V_{x}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})\to W_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}:V_{x}\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})\to W_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the invariance property of the canonical Whitney stratification of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant diffeomorphisms, the canonical Whitney stratification on the fibers can be “patched together” to define a canonical Whitney stratification on the fiber bundle <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> which is “locally trivial.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another statement relevant to us, which was originally proved by Fukaya–Ono, says that when <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, the variety <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a union of smooth pieces. For a proof, the readers could refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Proposition 4.9]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large, for each subgroup <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>, the locus</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})_{H}:=Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\cap({\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\cap{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})_{H}:=Z_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\cap({\bm{V}}_{H}^{*}\cap{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a smooth algebraic submanifold of complex dimension</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{C}}{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})+\dim_{\mathbb{C}}\mathring{\bm{V}}^{H}-\dim_{\mathbb{C}}\mathring{\bm{W}}^{H}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{C}}{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}({\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})+\dim_{\mathbb{C}}\mathring{\bm{V}}^{H}-\dim_{\mathbb{C}}\mathring{\bm{W}}^{H}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.3. </span>FOP sections and strong transversality</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we recall the notion of normally complex sections (which we call by FOP sections, owing credit to Fukaya–Ono and B. Parker), which is Parker’s generalization of the notion of normally polynomial sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider the case for a single chart. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth manifold, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite group trivially acting on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{V}:V\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{V}:V\to B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{W}:W\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{W}:W\to B</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant complex vector bundles. Choose a positive integer <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a vector bundle</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)\to B" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)\to B</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">whose fiber over each <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in B</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector space <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant polynomial maps with degree at most <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\epsilon}\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\epsilon}\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> be an open <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant disk subbundle with respect to an auxiliary bundle metric. Consider smooth sections of the pullback bundle <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{V}^{*}W\to V_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{V}^{*}W\to V_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:V_{\epsilon}\to\pi_{V}^{*}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:V_{\epsilon}\to\pi_{V}^{*}W</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant section.</p>
<ol id="S2.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normally polynomial section</span> of degree at most <math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> if its restriction to each fiber <math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}\cap V_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}\cap V_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the restriction of an element of <math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V_{x},W_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normally complex section</span> of degree at most <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> if for each <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)\in V_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)\in V_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a smooth <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant bundle map <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V_{\epsilon}\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V_{\epsilon}\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for points <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x^{\prime},v^{\prime})=f(x^{\prime},v^{\prime})(v^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x^{\prime},v^{\prime})=f(x^{\prime},v^{\prime})(v^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I5.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">local lift</span> of <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math>. We also call a normally complex section an <span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FOP section</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An FOP section <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S2.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">strongly transverse</span> at <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)\in V_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)\in V_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> if for any local lift <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph of <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, as a submanifold of the total space of <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\epsilon}\oplus{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\epsilon}\oplus{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, is transverse to the canonical Whitney stratification of <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the graph of <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to all the strata of the canonical Whitney (pre)stratification of <math id="S2.I5.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With the above preparations, now we consider the global situation for a normally complex derived orbifold chart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact normally complex derived orbifold chart equipped with a straightening.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A section <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called an <span id="S2.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FOP section</span> if for each bundle chart <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}},E,U)</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant section <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:U\to E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:U\to E</annotation></semantics></math>, the following condition is true. Over <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> we can decompose <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E|_{U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}=\mathring{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\oplus\check{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E|_{U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}=\mathring{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\oplus\check{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The straightening induces an identification of a tubular neighborhood of <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a disk bundle <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> inside the normal bundle <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as an equivariant bundle isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E|_{N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}\cong\pi_{NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}^{*}\mathring{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\oplus\pi_{NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}^{*}\check{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E|_{N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}\cong\pi_{NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}^{*}\mathring{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\oplus\pi_{NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}^{*}\check{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I6.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then with respect to this splitting we can decompose</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S|_{N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}=(\mathring{S},\check{S})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S|_{N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}=(\mathring{S},\check{S}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I6.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We require that <math id="S2.I6.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{S}:N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to\pi_{NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}^{*}\check{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>Γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{S}:N_{\epsilon}U^{\mathit{\Gamma}}\to\pi_{NU^{\mathit{\Gamma}}}^{*}\check{E}^{\mathit{\Gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an FOP section.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S2.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FOP perturbation</span> of <math id="S2.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth perturbation <math id="S2.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Definition 2.1. ‣ 2.1.1. Derived orbifold charts ‣ 2.1. Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on 𝒵_𝑑 ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) such that <math id="S2.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an FOP section near its zero locus.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An FOP section <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S2.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">strongly transverse</span> at <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> if the following conditions are satisfied. Let <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},U_{x},E_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x},U_{x},E_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> be a bundle chart centered at <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. By shrinking the chart we assume that <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with a disk bundle <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\epsilon}U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\epsilon}U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the normal bundle <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to an equivariant section <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{x}:U_{x}\to E_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{x}:U_{x}\to E_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. With respect to the splitting (induced from the straightening)</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{x}\cong\pi_{NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}}^{*}\mathring{E}_{x}\oplus\pi_{NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}}^{*}\check{E}_{x}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{x}\cong\pi_{NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}}^{*}\mathring{E}_{x}\oplus\pi_{NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}}^{*}\check{E}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I6.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we write <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{x}=(\mathring{S}_{x},\check{S}_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{x}=(\mathring{S}_{x},\check{S}_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we require that there exists an local lift <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:N_{\epsilon}U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:N_{\epsilon}U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{S}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{S}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the induced bundle map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathring{S}_{x},{\rm graph}f):N_{\epsilon}U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\to\mathring{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\oplus(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\oplus{\rm Poly}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathring{S}_{x},{\rm graph}f):N_{\epsilon}U_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\to\mathring{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\oplus(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}\oplus{\rm Poly}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I6.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is transverse to the subbundle <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0\}\oplus{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0\}\oplus{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the canonical Whitney stratification at the point <math id="S2.I6.i3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in U_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in U_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The strong transversality condition of FOP sections presented as above <em id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a priori</em> depends on the choices of bundle charts and the cut-off degree <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}}(NU_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}},\check{E}_{x}^{{\mathit{\Gamma}}_{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The most important output from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Section 4]</cite> is showing that the canonical Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G}(V,W)</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with the change of the cut-off degree <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the group <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, when interpreted suitably. The upshot is, once fixing a straightening datum of <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the strong transversality condition is in fact intrinsic. The readers could refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite> for details, and we continue our discussions with such background in mind.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is a general fact that generic smooth maps are transverse to a given Whitney stratified object. As a consequence, a generic FOP section is strongly transverse. We formulate this fact as the following Proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 6.4]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact normally complex derived orbifold chart equipped with a straightening. Fix a continuous norm on <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a precompact open neighborhood <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}\subset{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}\subset{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Absolute version)</span><span id="S2.I7.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Given </span><math id="S2.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists a smooth section </span><math id="S2.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{\epsilon}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{\epsilon}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfying the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I7.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an FOP section in a neighborhood of </span><math id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and it is strongly transverse near </span><math id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}_{\epsilon}\|_{C^{0}({\mathcal{D}})}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}_{\epsilon}\|_{C^{0}({\mathcal{D}})}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Relative version)</span><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> More generally, let </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a compact subset, </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}\subset{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}\subset{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be an open neighborhood of </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Suppose we are given a section </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{E}}|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{E}}|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which is an FOP section and strongly transverse near </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\cap\overline{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\cap\overline{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then there exists a smooth perturbation </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which is an FOP section and strongly transverse near </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, such that </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> coincides with </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> near </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In addition, if </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is sufficiently small and </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\|_{C^{0}}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\|_{C^{0}}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then we can choose </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S2.I7.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}\|_{C^{0}}&lt;2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\mathcal{S}}-{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}\|_{C^{0}}&lt;2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I7.i2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The relative version of the above proposition is often referred to as a “CUDV” type statement. This means that a good perturbation has been constructed on an open neighborhood <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of a closed subset <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and we would like to find a good perturbation on a neighborhood of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cup D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cup D</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is another closed subset, while we want to maintain the original perturbation near <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and do not change anything outside an open neighborhood <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\setminus C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\setminus C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, as proposed by Fukaya–Ono, the isotropy free part of the zero locus of a strongly transverse FOP section should induce a homology class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> be an oriented (i.e., both <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> are oriented) and compact normally complex derived orbifold chart equipped with a straightening. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}^{*}\subset{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}^{*}\subset{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the manifold part, i.e., the open and dense subset of points whose isotropy groups are trivial. Then the following is true.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S2.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a strongly transverse FOP perturbation. Then the set </span><math id="S2.I8.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an oriented smooth submanifold of </span><math id="S2.I8.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of real dimension being </span><math id="S2.I8.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm dim}{\mathcal{U}}-{\rm rank}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm dim}{\mathcal{U}}-{\rm rank}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the inclusion map </span><math id="S2.I8.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}^{*}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{S}}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}^{*}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I8.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an oriented pseudocycle, hence represents an integral homology class. </span><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                        <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                        
                        
                        
                      In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite> we extend the notion of pseudocycles in manifolds to general Thom–Mather stratified spaces (including orbifolds) and proved that they represent integral homology classes.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The resulting homology class, called the </span><span id="S2.I8.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FOP Euler class</span><span id="S2.I8.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, denoted by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\rm FOP}({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})\in H_{*}({\mathcal{U}};{\mathbb{Z}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>FOP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\rm FOP}({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})\in H_{*}({\mathcal{U}};{\mathbb{Z}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I8.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I8.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is independent of the choice of strongly transverse FOP perturbations and is independent of the choice of straightening, and hence is an invariant of the normally complex derived orbifold chart.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In fact there exist a collection of homology classes associated to a derived orbifold chart indexed by a finite group and a pair of complex representations. The FOP Euler class in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 2.11. ‣ 2.1.3. FOP sections and strong transversality ‣ 2.1. Orbifolds and the canonical Whitney stratification on 𝒵_𝑑 ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> is the leading one in this collection.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>FOP sections and products</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we prove that the product of strongly transverse FOP sections is still strongly transverse. This is a necessary ingredient for the inductive construction of perturbations in Floer theory. Moreover, we show that the natural transformation <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}OP}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}OP}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> from the stably complex derived orbifold bordism <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},{\rm der}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},{\rm der}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the integral homology constructed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> is multiplicative.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1. </span>Products of transverse FOP sections</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fix our notations. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> be finite groups. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{i},{\bm{W}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{i},{\bm{W}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be complex <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-representations. Then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}\times G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}\times G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> representations under the product action. Choose nonnegative integers <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,d_{1},d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,d_{1},d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq d_{1},d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq d_{1},d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the space</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It has a subspace</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Poly}_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1})\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Poly}_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1})\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">One also has the inclusion of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-varieties</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1})\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2})\subset Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1})\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2})\subset Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Abbreviate the three <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-varieties as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are sufficiently large, the inclusion</span></p>
<table id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\big({\bm{V}}_{1}\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1})\big)\times\big({\bm{V}}_{2}\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2})\big)\\
\hookrightarrow({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\big({\bm{V}}_{1}\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1})\big)\times\big({\bm{V}}_{2}\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2})\big)\\
\hookrightarrow({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is transverse to all the strata of the canonical Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the inclusion pulls back the canonical Whitney stratification on the target to the canonical (product) Whitney stratification on the domain.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is given in Subsection <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Proof of Proposition ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> be normally complex derived orbifold charts such that <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongly transverse FOP section. Then the product section</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}:{\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}:{\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is also a strongly transverse FOP section.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As transversality is defined locally, it suffices to restrict our consideration to local charts. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{i},E_{i},G_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{i},E_{i},G_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> be a bundle chart of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is pulled back to a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant section</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}:U_{i}\to E_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}:U_{i}\to E_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU_{i}^{G_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU_{i}^{G_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial with fiber <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial with fiber <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Decompose <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{i}=\mathring{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}}\oplus\check{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{i}=\mathring{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}}\oplus\check{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then near <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NU_{i}^{G_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NU_{i}^{G_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> we can write</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=(\mathring{S}_{i},\check{S}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=(\mathring{S}_{i},\check{S}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">By the definition of FOP sections, there exists a local lift of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{S}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{S}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> near the origin</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:U_{i}^{G_{i}}\times{\bm{V}}_{i}\to{\rm Poly}_{d_{i}}^{G_{i}}({\bm{V}}_{i},\check{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:U_{i}^{G_{i}}\times{\bm{V}}_{i}\to{\rm Poly}_{d_{i}}^{G_{i}}({\bm{V}}_{i},\check{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By assumption, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{S}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{S}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\mathring{\bm{W}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\mathring{\bm{W}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (in the usual sense). Denote <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=U_{1}\times U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=U_{1}\times U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}={\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}={\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\bm{W}}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}=\mathring{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{1}}\oplus\mathring{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\bm{W}}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}=\mathring{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{1}}\oplus\mathring{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{S}=(\mathring{S}_{1},\mathring{S}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{S}=(\mathring{S}_{1},\mathring{S}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\mathring{\bm{W}}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\mathring{\bm{W}}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{S}^{-1}(0)=\mathring{S}_{1}^{-1}(0)\times\mathring{S}_{2}^{-1}(0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{S}^{-1}(0)=\mathring{S}_{1}^{-1}(0)\times\mathring{S}_{2}^{-1}(0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence we may assume that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{1}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{1}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, the graph of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{G_{i}}\times Z_{d_{i}}^{G_{i}}({\bm{V}}_{i},\check{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{G_{i}}\times Z_{d_{i}}^{G_{i}}({\bm{V}}_{i},\check{\bm{W}}_{i}^{G_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm graph}(f)={\rm graph}(f_{1}\times f_{2})={\rm graph}(f_{1})\times{\rm graph}(f_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm graph}(f)={\rm graph}(f_{1}\times f_{2})={\rm graph}(f_{1})\times{\rm graph}(f_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that the graph of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to the product Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},\check{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{2}})\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},\check{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},\check{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{2}})\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},\check{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>. If we view <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as a map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times{\bm{V}}_{1}\times{\bm{V}}_{2}\to{\rm Poly}_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},\check{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{1}}\oplus\check{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times{\bm{V}}_{1}\times{\bm{V}}_{2}\to{\rm Poly}_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},\check{\bm{W}}_{1}^{G_{1}}\oplus\check{\bm{W}}_{2}^{G_{2}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">it is easy to see that the intersection between <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm graph}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm graph}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm13" title="Proposition 2.13. ‣ 2.2.1. Products of transverse FOP sections ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>, as the inclusion <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}\hookrightarrow Z_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}\hookrightarrow Z_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> respects the canonical Whitney stratifications, we see that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm graph}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm graph}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to all the strata of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}\times Z_{{\rm max}(d_{1},d_{2})}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, this means that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{1}\times S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{1}\times S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly transverse at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},x_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},x_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2. </span>Multiplicativity of the FOP natural transformation</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A consequence of previous discussions is that the FOP natural transformation defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite> is natural with respect to products. Although this result will not be used in the setting of Floer theory, we include it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us recall the relevant notations. An <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">isotropy type</span> is a triple <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite group, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite-dimensional complex <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-representations which do not contain trivial <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-summands. A <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stable isotropy type</span> is an equivalence classes of isotropy types with respect to the equivalence relation generated by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\sim(G,{\bm{V}}\oplus{\bm{R}},{\bm{W}}\oplus{\bm{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})\sim(G,{\bm{V}}\oplus{\bm{R}},{\bm{W}}\oplus{\bm{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nontrivial irreducible complex representation of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. A stable isotropy type is denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}}]</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> represents it. To discuss products, we define the multiplication of (stable) isotropy types in the obvious way:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[G_{1},{\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1}]\times[G_{2},{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2}]:=[G_{1}\times G_{2},{\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[G_{1},{\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1}]\times[G_{2},{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2}]:=[G_{1}\times G_{2},{\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The pseudocycles defined by strongly transverse FOP sections induce natural transformations of generalized homology theories. First, for a topological space <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> one can define the stably complex derived orbifold bordism group</p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">generated by isomorphism classed of quadruples <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact stably complex derived orbifold chart and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathcal{U}}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathcal{U}}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous map, modulo the equivalence relations generated by</p>
<ol id="S2.I9" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I9.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I9.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I9.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<span id="S2.I9.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Restriction</span>) <math id="S2.I9.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)\sim({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime},f^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)\sim({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime},f^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.I9.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}\subset{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}\subset{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open neighborhood of <math id="S2.I9.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I9.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime}={\mathcal{E}}|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime}={\mathcal{E}}|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I9.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}={\mathcal{S}}|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}={\mathcal{S}}|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I9.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}=f|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}=f|_{{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I9.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I9.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I9.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<span id="S2.I9.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stabilization</span>) <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)\sim({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime},f^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)\sim({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime},f^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the total space of a vector bundle <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime}=\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\oplus\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime}=\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\oplus\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}=\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{S}}\oplus\tau_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime}=\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{S}}\oplus\tau_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathcal{F}}\to\pi_{\mathcal{F}}^{*}{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological section, and <math id="S2.I9.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}=f\circ\pi_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}=f\circ\pi_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I9.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I9.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I9.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<span id="S2.I9.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cobordism</span>) <math id="S2.I9.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)\sim({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime},f^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}},f)\sim({\mathcal{U}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{E}}^{\prime},{\mathcal{S}}^{\prime},f^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a bordism between them extending the stable complex structures.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">A stable complex structure on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}},{\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> is roughly a lift of the virtual bundle <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{\mathcal{U}}-{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{\mathcal{U}}-{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a complex virtual bundle. Disjoint union of derived orbifold charts induces the group structure on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. For details of the relevant terminologies, the readers could refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Section 7]</cite>. For each stable isotropy type <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> represented by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,{\bm{V}},{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\gamma}={\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}{\bm{V}}-{\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dim</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>dim</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\gamma}={\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}{\bm{V}}-{\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>]</cite> we constructed a natural transformation of generalized homology theories made of linear maps (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>)</p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma}^{Y}:\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y)\to H_{*-n_{\gamma}}(Y;{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>γ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma}^{Y}:\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y)\to H_{*-n_{\gamma}}(Y;{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The main consequence of the fact that strong transversality of FOP perturbations is preserved after taking products regarding the stably complex derived bordism theory is the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be topological spaces and <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1},\gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1},\gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be stable isotropy types. Then the follow diagram is commutative:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.E2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="79.46" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-58.79342118882533px" version="1.1" viewBox="73.14 58.79 450.12 79.46" width="450.12"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 73.14411489007152 79.45823854177476) translate(0,3.54) translate(73.14,0)"><g transform="translate(-73.14,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.97em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="137.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle{\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y_{1})\times\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y_{2})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y_{1})\times\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y_{2})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 73.14411489007152 -0.27673578005007266 L 73.14411489007152 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(86.29,0) translate(0,10.33) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.36" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.9)" width="81.63"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{1}}^{Y_{1}}\times\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{2}}^{Y_{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{1}}^{Y_{1}}\times\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{2}}^{Y_{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(189.38,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 73.14411489007152 -0.27673578005007266 L 189.37491605237952 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 189.37491605237952 -0.27673578005007266 L 189.37491605237952 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(189.38,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:12.96em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="179.3"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H_{*-n_{\gamma_{1}}}(Y_{1};{\mathbb{Z}})\times H_{*-n_{\gamma_{2}}}(Y_{2};{\mathbb{Z}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H_{*-n_{\gamma_{1}}}(Y_{1};{\mathbb{Z}})\times H_{*-n_{\gamma_{2}}}(Y_{2};{\mathbb{Z}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 282.8978436508168 -13.221982473223242 L 283.45131521091696 -13.221982473223242" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(283.18,0) translate(0,-34.58)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 282.8978436508168 -34.57869206026446 L 283.45131521091696 -13.221982473223242" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 282.8978436508168 -34.57869206026446 L 283.45131521091696 -34.57869206026446" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-50.77,0) translate(0,-45.57) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="93.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle{{\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{\mathbb{C},{\rm der}}(Y_{1}\times Y_{2})}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{\mathbb{C},{\rm der}}(Y_{1}\times Y_{2})}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 50.76459982557813 -45.84817449565215 L 50.76459982557813 -45.29470293555201" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(97.73,0) translate(0,-35.24) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.36" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.9)" width="57.01"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\mathcal{FOP}^{Y_{1}\times Y_{2}}_{\gamma_{1}\times\gamma_{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.710em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.710em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\mathcal{FOP}^{Y_{1}\times Y_{2}}_{\gamma_{1}\times\gamma_{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(210.02,0) translate(0,-45.57)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 50.76459982557813 -45.84817449565215 L 210.01507273613706 -45.29470293555201" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 210.01507273613706 -45.84817449565215 L 210.01507273613706 -45.29470293555201" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(210.02,0) translate(0,-45.57) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.97em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.4em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="138.02"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H_{*-n_{\gamma_{1}}-n_{\gamma_{2}}}(Y_{1}\times Y_{2};{\mathbb{Z}}).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H_{*-n_{\gamma_{1}}-n_{\gamma_{2}}}(Y_{1}\times Y_{2};{\mathbb{Z}}).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here the left vertical arrow is the map induced by product of derived orbifold charts:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{1},{\mathcal{E}}_{1},{\mathcal{S}}_{1},f_{1})\times({\mathcal{U}}_{2},{\mathcal{E}}_{2},{\mathcal{S}}_{2},f_{2})\mapsto({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2},{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2},{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2},f_{1}\times f_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{1},{\mathcal{E}}_{1},{\mathcal{S}}_{1},f_{1})\times({\mathcal{U}}_{2},{\mathcal{E}}_{2},{\mathcal{S}}_{2},f_{2})\mapsto({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2},{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2},{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2},f_{1}\times f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the right vertical arrow is the Eilenberg–Zilber map.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i},f_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i},f_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> be a representative of an element of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>der</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Omega}{}_{*}^{{\mathbb{C}},\rm der}(Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. By the definition of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{i}}^{Y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{i}}^{Y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we choose straightenings on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and choose strongly transverse FOP sections <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{i}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{i}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{i}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{i}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a pseudocycle in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which is contained in the closure of the stratum <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{i,\gamma_{i}}\subset{\mathcal{U}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{i,\gamma_{i}}\subset{\mathcal{U}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the set of points <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> whose stabilizers are isomorphic to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and for a bundle chart <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{i},E_{i},U_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{i},E_{i},U_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the stable isotropy type defined by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{i},(NU^{G_{i}})_{x_{i}},(\check{E}_{i})_{x_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{i},(NU^{G_{i}})_{x_{i}},(\check{E}_{i})_{x_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the class <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{i}}^{Y_{i}}([{\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i},f_{i}])=(f_{i})_{*}[\overline{({\mathcal{S}}_{i}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap\mathcal{U}_{i,\gamma_{i}}}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{FOP}_{\gamma_{i}}^{Y_{i}}([{\mathcal{U}}_{i},{\mathcal{E}}_{i},{\mathcal{S}}_{i},f_{i}])=(f_{i})_{*}[\overline{({\mathcal{S}}_{i}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap\mathcal{U}_{i,\gamma_{i}}}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider the product chart <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2},{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2},{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2},f_{1}\times f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊞</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2},{\mathcal{E}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{E}}_{2},{\mathcal{S}}_{1}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2},f_{1}\times f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The chosen straightenings produce a straightening on the product, with respect to which the product <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊞</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an FOP section. Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmthm14" title="Corollary 2.14. ‣ 2.2.1. Products of transverse FOP sections ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a> implies that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊞</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a strongly transverse FOP section and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)=({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\times({\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊞</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)=({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\times({\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Restricting the product <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{1,\gamma_{1}}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2,\gamma_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{1,\gamma_{1}}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2,\gamma_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has, as sets</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2})_{\gamma_{1}\times\gamma_{2}}=\big(({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}_{1,\gamma_{1}}\big)\times\big(({\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}_{2,\gamma_{2}}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊞</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime}\boxplus{\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap({\mathcal{U}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{U}}_{2})_{\gamma_{1}\times\gamma_{2}}=\big(({\mathcal{S}}_{1}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}_{1,\gamma_{1}}\big)\times\big(({\mathcal{S}}_{2}^{\prime})^{-1}(0)\cap{\mathcal{U}}_{2,\gamma_{2}}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">As the product of pseudocycles is still a pseudocycle and the homology classes represented by pseudocycles respect such product structures, therefore the commutativity of the diagram (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Theorem 2.15. ‣ 2.2.2. Multiplicativity of the FOP natural transformation ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) follows. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm13" title="Proposition 2.13. ‣ 2.2.1. Products of transverse FOP sections ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First we consider the canonical Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Abbreviate</p>
<table id="S2.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}_{i}={\bm{V}}_{i}\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{i}}^{G_{i}}({\bm{V}}_{i},{\bm{W}}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}_{i}={\bm{V}}_{i}\times{\rm Poly}_{d_{i}}^{G_{i}}({\bm{V}}_{i},{\bm{W}}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is endowed with the action prestratification, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}_{i}=\bigsqcup_{H_{i}\in{\mathfrak{A}}_{i}}{\bm{V}}_{i,H_{i}}^{*}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}_{i}=\bigsqcup_{H_{i}\in{\mathfrak{A}}_{i}}{\bm{V}}_{i,H_{i}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We explain the notations. For each subgroup <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}\subset G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}\subset G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{i,H_{i}}^{*}\subset{\bm{V}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{i,H_{i}}^{*}\subset{\bm{V}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of points whose stabilizers are exactly <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The symbol <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{A}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{A}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all subgroups of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}_{i,H_{i}}^{*}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}_{i,H_{i}}^{*}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Then on the product <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}:={\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}:={\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which has the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}\times G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}\times G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, the strata of the action prestratification is indexed exactly by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{A}}_{1}\times{\mathfrak{A}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{A}}_{1}\times{\mathfrak{A}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}=\bigsqcup_{(H_{1},H_{2})\in{\mathfrak{A}}_{1}\times{\mathfrak{A}}_{2}}{\bm{V}}_{1,H_{1}}^{*}\times{\bm{V}}_{2,H_{2}}^{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}=\bigsqcup_{(H_{1},H_{2})\in{\mathfrak{A}}_{1}\times{\mathfrak{A}}_{2}}{\bm{V}}_{1,H_{1}}^{*}\times{\bm{V}}_{2,H_{2}}^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The strata of this prestratification are all algebraic submanifolds. Hence by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Theorem A.21]</cite>, the variety <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a canonical Whitney stratification, which is the minimal Whitney stratification respecting the action prestratification; by Proposition <a href="#A1.Thmthm6" title="Proposition A.6. ‣ A.3. Relative case ‣ Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a> of Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, this Whitney stratification is the product of the canonical Whitney stratifications on <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{1}}^{G_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d_{2}}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we prove Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm13" title="Proposition 2.13. ‣ 2.2.1. Products of transverse FOP sections ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>. As the canonical Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> respects the inclusion <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G}\hookrightarrow Z_{d^{\prime}}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G}\hookrightarrow Z_{d^{\prime}}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Theorem 4.12]</cite>), one may assume that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=d_{2}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=d_{2}=d</annotation></semantics></math>. The inclusion map sends <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the following lemma is analogous to that of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Lemma 4.14]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})\to{\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})\to{\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfying the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I10" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I10.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I10.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I10.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I10.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\circ\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\circ\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I10.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the identity map on </span><math id="S2.I10.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I10.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I10.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I10.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I10.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I10.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each </span><math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,P)\in({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,P)\in({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I10.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S2.I10.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}(\psi(v,P))={\rm ev}(v,P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}(\psi(v,P))={\rm ev}(v,P)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I10.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I10.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I10.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I10.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I10.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is transverse to </span><math id="S2.I10.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I10.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and pulls back the canonical Whitney stratification to itself.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, denote its <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-component by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and its <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-component by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can regard <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant polynomial map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\in{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\in{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">and regard <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant polynomial map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}\in{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}\in{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}}({\bm{V}}_{1},{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{2})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then for <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(v_{1},v_{2})\in{\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v_{1},v_{2})\in{\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(v,P)=\psi(v_{1},v_{2},P_{1},P_{2})=\Big((v_{1},P_{1}(\cdot,v_{2})),(v_{2},P_{2}(v_{1},\cdot))\Big)\in{\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(v,P)=\psi(v_{1},v_{2},P_{1},P_{2})=\Big((v_{1},P_{1}(\cdot,v_{2})),(v_{2},P_{2}(v_{1},\cdot))\Big)\in{\mathcal{Y}}_{1}\times{\mathcal{Y}}_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Then it is easy to verify that <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\circ\phi={\rm Id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\circ\phi={\rm Id}</annotation></semantics></math> and that</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}(\psi(v,P))={\rm ev}(v,P)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}(\psi(v,P))={\rm ev}(v,P).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we prove the last property. Consider the manifold <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B={\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B={\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> over which there are the trivial bundles <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=B\times({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=B\times({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=B\times({\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=B\times({\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there are two bundle maps</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{f}}_{1},\underline{\smash{f}}_{2}:V\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}(V,W)\cong B\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{f}}_{1},\underline{\smash{f}}_{2}:V\to{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}(V,W)\cong B\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where (here the first variable is the fiber coordinate and the second variable is the base coordinate)</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \underline{\smash{f}}_{1}(v,P)=(P,P),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \underline{\smash{f}}_{1}(v,P)=(P,P),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \underline{\smash{f}}_{2}(v,P)=(\underline{\smash{\phi}}(\psi(v,P)),P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \underline{\smash{f}}_{2}(v,P)=(\underline{\smash{\phi}}(\psi(v,P)),P)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\phi}}(v,P)=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\phi}}(v,P)=P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.3. Proof of Proposition ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) implies that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm graph}(\underline{\smash{f}}_{1}-\underline{\smash{f}}_{2})\in{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}(V,W)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm graph}(\underline{\smash{f}}_{1}-\underline{\smash{f}}_{2})\in{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}(V,W).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">As the identity map of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Poly</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2})\times{\rm Poly}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}({\bm{V}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{V}}_{2},{\bm{W}}_{1}\oplus{\bm{W}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that the graph of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{f}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{f}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, by a lemma of B. Parker (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par13</a>, Lemma 4.10]</cite>, also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Lemma 4.7]</cite>), the graph of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{f}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{f}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is also transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, as the identity map pulls back any Whitney stratification to itself, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Lemma 4.8]</cite>, the map <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math> also pulls back the canonical Whitney stratification on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm13" title="Proposition 2.13. ‣ 2.2.1. Products of transverse FOP sections ‣ 2.2. FOP sections and products ‣ 2. Recap of the FOP natural transformation and multiplicativity ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> along the image of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, it follows that <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}\times G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere. Moreover, as <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\circ\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\circ\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity, it follows that <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> along the image of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, as <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\psi\circ\phi\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\psi\circ\phi\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that the image of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\psi</annotation></semantics></math> at any point is equal to the image of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\psi</annotation></semantics></math> at some point in <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Im}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Im}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{d}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{d}^{G_{1}}\times Z_{d}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof of the claim that <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> pulls back the canonical Whitney stratification to the canonical one is similar to the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Theorem 4.12]</cite>, which relies crucially on a property of minimal Whitney stratifications (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BX22</a>, Lemma A.11]</cite>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we provide an abstract recipe of constructing chain complexes associated to flow categories and chain maps associated to flow bimodules. We explain the list of necessary structures on flow categories and flow bimodules which allow one to use FOP perturbations to define the algebraic counts over the integers. This section also serves as a source of notations. In Subsection <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Stratified spaces ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we set up the notations for partially ordered sets and abstract stratified spaces. In Subsection <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Topological flow categories and bimodules ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> we introduce the abstract notion of topological flow categories and flow bimodules. In Subsection <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Derived orbifold charts with stratifications ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> we define the notion of derived orbifold lifts of flow categoreis and bimodules which are abstract frameworks for regularizing the moduli spaces. In Subsection <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. Additional structures ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we lift certain auxiliary structures which will be necessary to carry out the FOP perturbation scheme. In Subsection <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6. Normal complex structure and orientations on flow categories ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> we consider the important notion of stable normal complex structures. In Subsection <a href="#S3.SS7" title="3.7. Constructing FOP perturbations ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> and Subsection <a href="#S3.SS8" title="3.8. Chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> we explain the recipe of inductively constructing FOP perturbations associated to derived orbifold lifts of flow categories and flow bimodules and the recipe of extracting chain complexes and chain maps from the countings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Stratified spaces</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>Partially ordered sets</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many objects in Floer theory are indexed by certain partially ordered sets. We abbreviate the phrase “partially ordered set” by the word <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">poset</span>. In this paper posets are always countable. We use different symbols such as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\preceq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⪯</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\preceq</annotation></semantics></math>, etc. to denote the partial order relations. For a poset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{\max}\subseteq{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>max</mi></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{\max}\subseteq{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of maximal elements. A poset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a canonical <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alexandrov topology</span>: a subset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is open if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq\beta</annotation></semantics></math> imply that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The product of finitely many posets carries a canonically induced partial order. Indeed, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{A}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{A}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are posets, then the relation</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{k})\leq(\beta_{1},\ldots,\beta_{k})\ \text{if and only if}\ \alpha_{i}\leq\beta_{i}\ \forall i=1,\ldots,k" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>if and only if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{k})\leq(\beta_{1},\ldots,\beta_{k})\ \text{if and only if}\ \alpha_{i}\leq\beta_{i}\ \forall i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is a partial order on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{A}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{A}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We often consider posets with a well-defined “depth” function. We introduce the following notion of homogeneous posets. In fact all moduli spaces considered in this paper are stratified by the following kind of posets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A poset <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">homogeneous</span> if for each <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the length of a maximal sequence of elements <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha_{0}&lt;\alpha_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha_{0}&lt;\alpha_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{k}\in{\mathbb{A}}^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>max</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{k}\in{\mathbb{A}}^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and only depends on <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. This length is called the <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">depth</span> or <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">codimension</span> of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm depth}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm depth}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}^{\max}\Longleftrightarrow{\rm depth}(\alpha)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>max</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}^{\max}\Longleftrightarrow{\rm depth}(\alpha)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A poset map between homogeneous posets is a called a <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">homogeneous</span> poset map if it preserves the depth.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a homogeneous poset <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">set of adjacent faces</span> of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{\alpha}:=\{\beta\in{\mathbb{A}}|\alpha\leq\beta\text{ and }{\rm depth}(\beta)=1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>depth</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{\alpha}:=\{\beta\in{\mathbb{A}}|\alpha\leq\beta\text{ and }{\rm depth}(\beta)=1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is straightforward to check that homogeneous posets also admit finite products with depth function being</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm depth}(\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{k})={\rm depth}(\alpha_{1})+\cdots+{\rm depth}(\alpha_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm depth}(\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{k})={\rm depth}(\alpha_{1})+\cdots+{\rm depth}(\alpha_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We introudce the following “boundary stratum” notation. Given a poset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and an element <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\alpha}{\mathbb{A}}:=\{\alpha^{\prime}\in{\mathbb{A}}\ |\ \alpha^{\prime}\leq\alpha\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\alpha}{\mathbb{A}}:=\{\alpha^{\prime}\in{\mathbb{A}}\ |\ \alpha^{\prime}\leq\alpha\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">More generally, if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset, denote</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\mathbb{B}}{\mathbb{A}}:=\bigcup_{\beta\in{\mathbb{B}}}\partial^{\beta}{\mathbb{A}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>β</mi></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\mathbb{B}}{\mathbb{A}}:=\bigcup_{\beta\in{\mathbb{B}}}\partial^{\beta}{\mathbb{A}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">It has the induced partial order. If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homogeneous poset, then <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\alpha}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\alpha}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also homogeneous with depth function being shifted by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm depth}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm depth}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, for each nonnegative integer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, denote</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{[k]}{\mathbb{A}}:=\partial^{{\rm depth}^{-1}(k)}{\mathbb{A}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>depth</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{[k]}{\mathbb{A}}:=\partial^{{\rm depth}^{-1}(k)}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">which is still homogeneous with the depth function shifted by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span>Stratified spaces</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce our notion of stratified topological spaces. We emphasize here that stratified spaces always refer to a poset. Moreover, the notion of stratified spaces should not be confused with the notion of prestratified spaces discussed in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Product of canonical Whitney stratifications ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a (countable) poset. An <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-stratified space</span> (<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>-space for short) is a locally compact, Hausdorff and second countable topological space <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> endowed with a continuous map</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:X\to{\mathbb{A}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:X\to{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with respect to the Alexandrov topology on <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the range of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. In particular, we can write</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\bigsqcup_{\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}}X_{\alpha},\ {\rm where}\ X_{\alpha}:=s^{-1}(\alpha)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\bigsqcup_{\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}}X_{\alpha},\ {\rm where}\ X_{\alpha}:=s^{-1}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">satisfying the following conditions.</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> (called a <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stratum</span>) is locally closed (which can be empty<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                        <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                        
                        
                        
                      For example, in a moduli space of stable Floer cylinders, the subset of smooth Floer cylinders, which should be the top stratum, could be empty.</span></span></span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All but finitely many strata are empty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the subset</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\alpha}X:=\bigsqcup_{\beta\leq\alpha}X_{\beta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\alpha}X:=\bigsqcup_{\beta\leq\alpha}X_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a closed set (which may contain the closure of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> properly). Note that this condition follows from the continuity of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the following notions for stratified spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A map from an <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-space <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to an <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-space <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative diagram</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="74.98" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-58.06730383079511px" version="1.1" viewBox="18.93 58.07 98.12 74.98" width="98.12"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 18.92507892765411 74.9791600454459) translate(0,3.46) translate(18.93,0)"><g transform="translate(-13.53,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="18.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{X_{1}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{X_{1}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 13.529522976176578 -0.27673578005007266 L 13.529522976176578 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(22.74,0) translate(0,8.46) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(46.74,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 13.529522976176578 -0.27673578005007266 L 46.73830219467794 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 46.73830219467794 -0.27673578005007266 L 46.73830219467794 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -9.685900096859001 L 0.27673578005007266 -9.685900096859001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-18.93,0) translate(0,-21.68) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-1.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.55" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="10.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{s_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{s_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -9.685900096859001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(46.74,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="18.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{X_{2}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{X_{2}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 59.99106827721781 -9.685900096859001 L 60.544539837317956 -9.685900096859001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(60.27,0) translate(0,-21.68) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-1.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.55" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="10.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{s_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{s_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(60.27,0) translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 59.99106827721781 -33.208800332088 L 60.544539837317956 -9.685900096859001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 59.99106827721781 -33.208800332088 L 60.544539837317956 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-12.44,0) translate(0,-43.36) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="16.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 12.443714556199842 -43.63264139549389 L 12.443714556199842 -43.07916983539375" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(24.03,0) translate(0,-50.71) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.28em;--ltx-fo-height:0.46em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.41)" width="3.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(47.82,0) translate(0,-43.36)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 12.443714556199842 -43.63264139549389 L 47.824089501068045 -43.07916983539375" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 47.824089501068045 -43.63264139549389 L 47.824089501068045 -43.07916983539375" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(47.82,0) translate(0,-43.36) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="16.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\mathbb{A}}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\mathbb{A}}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\to{\mathbb{A}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\to{\mathbb{A}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a poset map and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous map. If <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are both homogeneous (see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1.1. Partially ordered sets ‣ 3.1. Stratified spaces ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), then we require that <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a homogeneous map. We usually call such a map a <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stratified map</span> to emphasize that it respects the stratifications.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A stratified map <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X_{1}\to X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X_{1}\to X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as above is called an <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">embedding</span> if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a homeomorphism onto its image and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\to{\mathbb{A}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\to{\mathbb{A}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is an injection. An open embedding is an embedding with an open image.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A stratified map <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X_{1}\to X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X_{1}\to X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a homeomorphism if <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a homeomorphism of topological spaces and the underlying poset map is an isomorphism.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The product of <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-spaces <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>) is the product topological space <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{1}\times\cdots\times X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\times\cdots\times X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> stratified by the product poset <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{A}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{A}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to see</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial^{(\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{k})}(X_{1}\times\cdots\times X_{k})=\partial^{\alpha_{1}}X_{1}\times\cdots\times\partial^{\alpha_{k}}X_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msup><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">×</mo><msup><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{(\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{k})}(X_{1}\times\cdots\times X_{k})=\partial^{\alpha_{1}}X_{1}\times\cdots\times\partial^{\alpha_{k}}X_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a topological group. A <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action on an <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>-space <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which preserves each stratum. In this case, the <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\alpha}{\mathbb{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\alpha}{\mathbb{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>-space <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\alpha}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\alpha}X</annotation></semantics></math> has an induced <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Topological flow categories and bimodules</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The concept of flow categories was introduced by Cohen–Jones–Segal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CJS95</a>]</cite>. We need a variant of the original construction similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>, Section 7]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abo22</a>, Section 7,8]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_setup">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonnegative integer, <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> be an infinite cyclic group, and <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega:\Pi\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega:\Pi\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a group injection.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a countable poset equipped with the following extra data: a free <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>-action and two functions (called the </span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">action</span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the </span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">index</span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">)</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}:{\mathcal{P}}\to{\mathbb{R}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}:{\mathcal{P}}\to{\mathbb{R}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\rm ind}^{\mathcal{P}}:{\mathcal{P}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}/2N." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ind</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\rm ind}^{\mathcal{P}}:{\mathcal{P}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}/2N.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-action is order-preserving. Namely, for all </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq q\Longleftrightarrow a\cdot p\leq a\cdot q." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq q\Longleftrightarrow a\cdot p\leq a\cdot q.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(a\cdot p)={\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(p)+\omega(a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(a\cdot p)={\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(p)+\omega(a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ind}^{\mathcal{P}}(a\cdot p)=\rm{ind}^{\mathcal{P}}(p)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ind</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ind</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ind}^{\mathcal{P}}(a\cdot p)=\rm{ind}^{\mathcal{P}}(p).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q\Longrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(p)&lt;{\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(q)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q\Longrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(p)&lt;{\mathcal{A}}^{\mathcal{P}}(q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The quotient set </span><math id="S3.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathcal{P}}}:={\mathcal{P}}/\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathcal{P}}}:={\mathcal{P}}/\Pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The conditions that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega:\Pi\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega:\Pi\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective and that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathcal{P}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathcal{P}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite imply that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is “locally finite-dimensional,” namely, for any pair of elements <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at most finitely many elements lying between them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a pair of elements <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a poset</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}:=\big\{\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\ |\ p&lt;r_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;r_{l}&lt;q,\ r_{1},\ldots,r_{l}\in{\mathcal{P}}\big\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}:=\big\{\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\ |\ p&lt;r_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;r_{l}&lt;q,\ r_{1},\ldots,r_{l}\in{\mathcal{P}}\big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whose partial order is induced by inclusion</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="ps_{1}\cdots s_{m}q\leq pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\Longleftrightarrow\{r_{1}\cdots r_{l}\}\subseteq\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{m}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ps_{1}\cdots s_{m}q\leq pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\Longleftrightarrow\{r_{1}\cdots r_{l}\}\subseteq\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{m}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The poset <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique maximal element <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq</annotation></semantics></math> and is homogeneous whose depth function is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm depth}(pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q)=l." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>depth</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm depth}(pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q)=l.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following is a formal characterization of the feature that boundary strata of moduli spaces consist of broken trajectories. Namely, given a triple <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="prq\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">prq\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to see that there is an isomorphism of homogeneous posets</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{pr}^{\mathcal{P}}\times{\mathbb{A}}_{rq}^{\mathcal{P}}\cong\partial^{prq}{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{pr}^{\mathcal{P}}\times{\mathbb{A}}_{rq}^{\mathcal{P}}\cong\partial^{prq}{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">under the concatenation of strings.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now we introduce the notion of flow categories under the setting of Setup <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Setup 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Topological flow categories and bimodules ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in Setup <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Setup 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Topological flow categories and bimodules ‣ 3. Abstract constructions of chain complexes and maps over the integers ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. A <span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">flow category</span> <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a topologically enriched category<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
              Namely, the set of morphisms are topological spaces and composition maps are continuous.</span></span></span> with the set of objects given by <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with morphism spaces <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following conditions.</p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> only if <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                      <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                      
                      
                      
                    In the Morse or Floer case, it is indeed true that <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pp}</annotation></semantics></math> is the singleton.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact <math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>-space.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a triple <math id="S3.I4.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;r&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;r&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.I4.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the composition map factors through a stratified homeomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex19.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="66.40" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-56.094429181854935px" version="1.1" viewBox="32.77 56.09 153.83 66.40" width="153.83"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 32.76632289693813 66.39530471668742) translate(0,3.46) translate(32.77,0)"><g transform="translate(-32.77,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.14em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="57.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{T_{pr}\times T_{rq}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{T_{pr}\times T_{rq}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 32.76634401052477 -0.27673578005007266 L 32.76634401052477 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(66.16,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 32.76634401052477 -0.27673578005007266 L 66.16009936155217 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 66.16009936155217 -0.27673578005007266 L 66.16009936155217 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-32.19)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.193279042025566 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.193279042025566 L 0.27673578005007266 -32.193279042025566" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(66.16,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.35em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.57" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="46.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\partial^{prq}T_{pq}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\partial^{prq}T_{pq}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#00000