</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm53" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm53.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation 5.53</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm53.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm53.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariantization of the embedding <math id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex205" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex205.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta_{\alpha}^{\sim}:G_{pq}\times_{G_{\alpha}}V_{\alpha}\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta_{\alpha}^{\sim}:G_{pq}\times_{G_{\alpha}}V_{\alpha}\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and its image is denoted by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex206" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex206.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}^{\sim}\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}^{\sim}\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Points in <math id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> are those maps whose underlying map into <math id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>-normal position and whose obstruction vector are coming from factors of <math id="S5.Thmthm53.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.4. </span>More on obstruction bundles</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we single out a particular pattern of the obstruction bundles which will be used several times in the rest of the paper. We fix <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq d_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq d_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has part of the obstruction bundles</p>
<table id="S5.Ex207" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex207.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pr_{1}}^{(d)}\to V_{pr_{1}},\ldots,O_{r_{l}q}^{(d)}\to V_{r_{l}q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pr_{1}}^{(d)}\to V_{pr_{1}},\ldots,O_{r_{l}q}^{(d)}\to V_{r_{l}q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Via the above product construction, denote their images by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex208" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex208.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="O_{pr_{1}\to pq}^{(d)},\ldots,O_{r_{l}q\to pq}^{(d)}\to V_{\alpha}=V_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times V_{r_{l}q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pr_{1}\to pq}^{(d)},\ldots,O_{r_{l}q\to pq}^{(d)}\to V_{\alpha}=V_{pr_{1}}\times\cdots\times V_{r_{l}q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">They are embedded into the bundle <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote their (direct) sum by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex209" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex209.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\alpha}^{(d)}\to V_{\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\alpha}^{(d)}\to V_{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Notice that inside <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> there are also subbundles which are embedded images of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\beta}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\beta}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all intermediate strata <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In many inductive constructions, we wouldl like to choose structures (such as inner products, connections, maps, etc.) on <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> inductively. When we make this choice over a particular stratum <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}\subset V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}\subset V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, we would like to extend the existing structures on those subbundles coming from product construction. These subbundles have overlaps and the existing structures agree on overlaps by the correct inductive hypothesis. However, this does not guarantee the existence of an extension; indeed some extra properties of these subbundles <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\beta}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\beta}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> is needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm54" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm54.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.54</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm54.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm54.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm54.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm54.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S5.Thmthm54.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the two subbundles</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex210" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex210.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}\to V_{pr_{l}q}\subset O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{pr_{l}q}},O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}\to V_{pr_{1}q}\subset O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{pr_{1}q}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}\to V_{pr_{l}q}\subset O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{pr_{l}q}},O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}\to V_{pr_{1}q}\subset O_{pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{pr_{1}q}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<ol id="S5.I21" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I21.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I21.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I21.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I21.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One has</span></p>
<table id="S5.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}\cap O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}=O_{r_{1}r_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}\cap O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}=O_{r_{1}r_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I21.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I21.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I21.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I21.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each </span><math id="S5.I21.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I21.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S5.I21.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I21.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there one has</span></p>
<table id="S5.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\beta}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}\subset O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}+O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\beta}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}\subset O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}+O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}|_{V_{\alpha}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first consider the single-layered case. Choose a point <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{pr_{1}r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{pr_{1}r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math> with underlying curve <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that vectors in <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in H^{0}(Y)\otimes\overline{H^{0}(L)_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in H^{0}(Y)\otimes\overline{H^{0}(L)_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hermitian vector bundle and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a holomorphic line bundle. Let <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{r_{1}r_{l}}\subset\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{r_{1}r_{l}}\subset\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of components “between” <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a vector in the intersection (<a href="#S5.E43" title="In item 1 ‣ Lemma 5.54. ‣ 5.4.4. More on obstruction bundles ‣ 5.4. Global chart construction III ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.43</span></a>) is <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in H^{0}(Y)\otimes\overline{H^{0}(L)_{r_{1}r_{l}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in H^{0}(Y)\otimes\overline{H^{0}(L)_{r_{1}r_{l}}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(L)_{r_{1}r_{l}}\subset H^{0}(L)_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(L)_{r_{1}r_{l}}\subset H^{0}(L)_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subspace of holomorphic sections supported in <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{r_{1}r_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{r_{1}r_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math> which vanish at the corresponding two nodes. On the other hand, recall <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\overline{\rm Hom}(\iota_{F}^{*}T\mathbb{CP}^{d},u^{*}TM)\otimes\iota_{F}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Hom</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>TM</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\overline{\rm Hom}(\iota_{F}^{*}T\mathbb{CP}^{d},u^{*}TM)\otimes\iota_{F}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. As the underlying curve inside <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is in normal position, there is a corresponding fan <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{x,pr_{1}},W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}},W_{x,r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{x,pr_{1}},W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}},W_{x,r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the normal position. The condition <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in O_{pr_{l}\to pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in O_{pr_{l}\to pq}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on the normal direction of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{x,pr_{1}}+W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{x,pr_{1}}+W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the condition <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in O_{r_{1}\to pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in O_{r_{1}\to pq}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on the normal direction of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}}+W_{x,r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}}+W_{x,r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> vanshes on the normal direction of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{x,r_{1}r_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the way we define the inclusion <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{r_{1}r_{l}\to pq}\hookrightarrow O_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{r_{1}r_{l}\to pq}\hookrightarrow O_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in O_{r_{1}r_{l}\to pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in O_{r_{1}r_{l}\to pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves (<a href="#S5.E43" title="In item 1 ‣ Lemma 5.54. ‣ 5.4.4. More on obstruction bundles ‣ 5.4. Global chart construction III ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.43</span></a>) in the single layered case. The multi-layered case, more generally, can be proved by induction on the number of layers.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (<a href="#S5.E44" title="In item 2 ‣ Lemma 5.54. ‣ 5.4.4. More on obstruction bundles ‣ 5.4. Global chart construction III ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.44</span></a>), suppose <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=ps_{1},\ldots,s_{m}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=ps_{1},\ldots,s_{m}q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\geq r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\geq r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}\leq r_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}\leq r_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence each summand of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\beta}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\beta}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> i.e., one of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{ps_{1}\to pq}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{ps_{1}\to pq}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{s_{m}q\to pq}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{s_{m}q\to pq}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is either contained in <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pr_{l}\to pq}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> or in <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{r_{1}q\to pq}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Stabilization maps</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we state our proposition regarding the stabilization maps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm55" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm55.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.55</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm55.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm55.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm55.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm55.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S5.Thmthm55.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm55.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist the following objects.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I22" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I22.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I22.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each </span><math id="S5.I22.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, a </span><math id="S5.I22.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant subbundle </span><math id="S5.I22.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{pq,\alpha}\subset E_{pq}|_{V_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{pq,\alpha}\subset E_{pq}|_{V_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which is complementary to </span><math id="S5.I22.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and carries a stratification by </span><math id="S5.I22.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-invariant subbundles</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex211" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex211.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\beta\alpha}\ \forall\beta\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer},\alpha\leq\beta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\beta\alpha}\ \forall\beta\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer},\alpha\leq\beta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I22.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex212" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex212.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}\oplus F_{\beta\alpha}=E_{\beta}|_{V_{\alpha}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}\oplus F_{\beta\alpha}=E_{\beta}|_{V_{\alpha}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I22.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I22.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I22.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A germ of </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant embedding</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex213" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex213.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{pq,\alpha}:{\rm Stab}_{F_{pq,\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{pq,\alpha}:{\rm Stab}_{F_{pq,\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I22.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfying the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I22.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariantization is a germ of open embedding.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its restriction to </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (the zero section) coincides with the embedding </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{pq,\alpha}:V_{\alpha}\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{pq,\alpha}:V_{\alpha}\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each stratum </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> between </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I22.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, one has</span></p>
<table id="S5.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{pq,\alpha}\left({\rm Stab}_{F_{\beta\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\right)\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{\beta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{pq,\alpha}\left({\rm Stab}_{F_{\beta\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\right)\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact we would like the maps constructed to have better properties. The discussion here is to showcase the analytical part of the construction. The above proposition is enough to establish the construction at this moment, i.e., Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm62" title="Theorem 5.62. ‣ 5.6. Weak K-chart lift ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.62</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.5.1. </span>The difference bundle</h4>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first construct the bundle appeared in the stabilization map. Recall that <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{pq}\to V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{pq}\to V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally decomposed into <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> where the latter is a trivial bundle. The embedding <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{pq,\alpha}:V_{\alpha}\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{pq,\alpha}:V_{\alpha}\to\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is covered by a bundle embedding <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\alpha}\to O_{pq}|_{\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\alpha}\to O_{pq}|_{\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can take a <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant inner product on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the orthogonal complement <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{pq,\alpha}:=(O_{\alpha})^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{pq,\alpha}:=(O_{\alpha})^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, we have already chosen a good complement of <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\alpha}\subset Q_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\alpha}\subset Q_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{pq,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{pq,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> (which is trivial). Define</p>
<table id="S5.Ex214" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex214.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{pq,\alpha}:=O_{pq,\alpha}\oplus Q_{pq,\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{pq,\alpha}:=O_{pq,\alpha}\oplus Q_{pq,\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.5.2. </span>Analytical setup</h4>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As expected, the stabilization map is constructed using the infinite-dimensional implicit function theorem in a global fashion. We need a global analytical setup for this operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq</annotation></semantics></math> and abbreviate <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each point <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, its fiber in the universal curve is a well-defined prestable cylinder <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over which there is a holomorphic map <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mu}}:\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mu}}:\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a fiber bundle over <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\times M</annotation></semantics></math> denoted by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex215" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex215.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}\to\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\times M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}\to\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\times M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">More explicitly, in the single-layered case,</p>
<table id="S5.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}=\overline{\rm Hom}(\underline{\smash{\mu}}^{*}T\mathbb{CP}^{d},TM)\otimes\underline{\smash{\mu}}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(k)\otimes\overline{H^{0}(\underline{\smash{\mu}}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(k))_{0}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Hom</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>TM</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}=\overline{\rm Hom}(\underline{\smash{\mu}}^{*}T\mathbb{CP}^{d},TM)\otimes\underline{\smash{\mu}}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(k)\otimes\overline{H^{0}(\underline{\smash{\mu}}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(k))_{0}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">If we fix the point <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the fiber <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{pq}^{-1}(\underline{\smash{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{pq}^{-1}(\underline{\smash{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the space of sections <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\eta):\Sigma\to E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\eta):\Sigma\to E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a map and <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a section of <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}|_{{\rm graph}(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}|_{{\rm graph}(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now fix a positive integer <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{l}(x):=C^{l}(\Sigma,E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{l}(x):=C^{l}(\Sigma,E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)})</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>-sections <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\eta):\Sigma\to E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\eta):\Sigma\to E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we impose the asymptotic constraints at nodes and marked points to periodic orbits. Take the union over <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtained a well-defined topological space, denoted by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{l}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{l}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is also stratified by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now notice that one has a <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}^{l}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}^{l}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and an invariant subspace</p>
<table id="S5.Ex216" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex216.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{pq}=V_{pq}^{(d)}\subset{\mathcal{C}}^{l}_{pq}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{pq}=V_{pq}^{(d)}\subset{\mathcal{C}}^{l}_{pq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In fact, the complexification <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}^{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}^{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> by transforming the underlying points <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the maps <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>, although <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now we consider the difference between <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. We regard both as subspaces of the infinite-dimensional object, i.e.</p>
<table id="S5.Ex217" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex217.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}\subset\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}\subset\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We define an <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">approximate solution map</span></p>
<table id="S5.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{pq,\alpha}^{\rm app}:{\rm Stab}_{F_{pq,\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\to\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>app</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{pq,\alpha}^{\rm app}:{\rm Stab}_{F_{pq,\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\to\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex218" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex218.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma,u,\mu,\eta_{\alpha},\eta_{pq,\alpha},h_{pq,\alpha}]\mapsto({\rm Id}+\rho_{h_{pq,\alpha}})[\Sigma,u,\mu,\eta_{\alpha}+\eta_{pq,\alpha}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Id</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma,u,\mu,\eta_{\alpha},\eta_{pq,\alpha},h_{pq,\alpha}]\mapsto({\rm Id}+\rho_{h_{pq,\alpha}})[\Sigma,u,\mu,\eta_{\alpha}+\eta_{pq,\alpha}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{h_{pq,\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{h_{pq,\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper-triangular matrix defined in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm24" title="Proposition 5.24. ‣ 5.2.5. Boundary strata of the auxiliary moduli spaces ‣ 5.2. Global chart construction I ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.24</span></a>. The matrix <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Id}+\rho_{h_{pq,\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Id</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Id}+\rho_{h_{pq,\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the framing map <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mu}}:\Sigma\to{\mathbb{C}P}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mu}}:\Sigma\to{\mathbb{C}P}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and the obstruction sections. This is a <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map and satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.Ex219" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex219.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{pq,\alpha}^{\rm app}\left({\rm Stab}_{F_{\beta\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\right)\subset\partial^{\beta}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>app</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>β</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{pq,\alpha}^{\rm app}\left({\rm Stab}_{F_{\beta\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\right)\subset\partial^{\beta}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">However, it is not necessarily an exact solution, because the extra <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{pq,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{pq,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> can perturb the equation and the equation is not invariant under the complexification of <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to use right inverses to correct the solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm56" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm56.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.56</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm56.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm56.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm56.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When we construct stabilization maps inductively, the approximate solution map may not be just the linear addition in the fiber direction. In fact we may also need to deform the map <math id="S5.Thmthm56.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We first discuss the notion of linearizations of the defining equation of the thickened moduli space more carefully. Fix a real number <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix an element <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm57" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm57.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.57</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm57.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm57.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has the Banach manifold <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1,a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1,a}</annotation></semantics></math>-sections <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\eta):\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\to E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\eta):\Sigma_{\underline{\smash{x}}}\to E_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> which are asymptotic to prescribed periodic orbits and a Banach bundle <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\to{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\to{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of corresponding inhomogeneous terms of class <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here the Banach norms are defined with respect to the cylindrical metric on cylindrical components and arbitrary smooth metric on spherical components.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm57.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each point <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\pi_{pq}^{-1}(\underline{\smash{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\pi_{pq}^{-1}(\underline{\smash{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the linearization of the defining equation at <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a Fredholm operator</p>
<table id="S5.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{pq,x}:T_{x}{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}|_{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{pq,x}:T_{x}{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}|_{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By the regularity statement Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm49" title="Proposition 5.49. ‣ 5.4.1. Inductive construction of the thickened moduli spaces ‣ 5.4. Global chart construction III ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.49</span></a>, the linearizations are always surjective in our setting. Then we can talk about bounded right inverses. A right inverse of <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{pq,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{pq,x}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq,x}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq</annotation></semantics></math> is understood, we use <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm57.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> instead.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain global stabilization maps using the implicit function theorem, we need to choose a family of right inverses to the linearization. Obviously, we need a continuity condition. We define the notion of continuity by comparing with the local families obtained from the standard gluing procedure. Let us briefly recall the gluing construction in the context of pseudoholomorphic curves which should be most familiar to the reader. Given a nodal <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic curve <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,u)</annotation></semantics></math> with a stable domain <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> therefore finite automorphism group, there exists a local universal unfolding of the domain <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. There is also a way to identify any compact subset of the nodal <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> away from the nodes with a compact subset of nearby fibers. Choose a right inverse <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> at the point represented by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,u)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then using such identifications and certain cut-and-paste procedure, one can obtain a family of approximate right inverses for nearby solutions. For details, the reader could consult <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>, Equation (C.7.7)]</cite> for instance. These approximate right inverses determine exact right inverses which have the same images, as one could read off from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>, Equation (C.7.30)]</cite>. If <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter in a neighborhood of the point represented by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,u)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we denote by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\zeta}^{\rm glue}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mi>glue</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\zeta}^{\rm glue}</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction further depends on some auxiliary data including Riemannian metrics which define the parallel transport to compare the deformation space between the central curve and nearby curves, and a cut-off function which is needed for the construction of the pre-gluing map <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>, Equation (C.7.9)]</cite>. These data are referred to as a <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gluing profile</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Similar construction applies to our current situation for the thickened moduli spaces <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, as the spaces <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are locally isomorphic to the moduli space <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,d^{\prime}+2}^{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,d^{\prime}+2}^{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,the space of stable marked cylinders we can compare nearby fibers of the fiberwise Banach manifolds and identify compact subsets away from nodes with subsets of nearby fibers. The corresponding gluing construction can be performed in a similar way. The reader could consult Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm22" title="Proposition 6.22. ‣ 6.2.1. Topological submersions ‣ 6.2. Pre-smoothing operations and stable complex structures ‣ 6. Smoothing ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.22</span></a> for more arguments along this line.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm58" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm58.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.58</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm58.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Continuous family of right inverses)<span id="S5.Thmthm58.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm58.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A family of right inverses <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{x}\}_{x\in V_{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{x}\}_{x\in V_{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">continuous</span> at <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> if the following is true. On one hand, for each <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and any representative <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}=(\Sigma,u,\mu,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}=(\Sigma,u,\mu,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to each irreducible component of the domain defines a right inverse for the restriction of the linearized Cauchy–Riemann operator. Then given a gluing profile for <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, these right inverses induce a right inverse <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\zeta}^{\rm glue}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mi>glue</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\zeta}^{\rm glue}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a family of right inverses <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math> over this open neighborhood. Then for each compact set <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\Sigma\setminus\{{\rm nodes}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>nodes</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\Sigma\setminus\{{\rm nodes}\}</annotation></semantics></math> there holds</p>
<table id="S5.Ex220" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex220.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\zeta\to 0}\|T_{\zeta}^{\rm glue}-T_{\zeta}\|_{Z}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mi>glue</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\zeta\to 0}\|T_{\zeta}^{\rm glue}-T_{\zeta}\|_{Z}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here we use the operator norm on the domain <math id="S5.Thmthm58.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm59" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm59.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.59</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm59.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm59.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm59.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We left to the reader to check that the above notion of continuity is intrinsic, i.e., independent of the choice of the gluing profile. In fact one only needs to check that the family of right inverse obtained from one gluing profile is continuous with respect to another gluing profile.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm60" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm60.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.60</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm60.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm60.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each pair <math id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant continuous family of right inverses <math id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.Thmthm60.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to construct a continuous family of right inverses without the equivariance condition as we can average over the symmetry group <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, as any convex linear combination of two right inverses is still a right inverse, we only needs to construct continuous families locally and patch together using a continuous partition of unity. From the definition we know the local family of right inverses given by a gluing construction is locally continuous.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let us fix a choice of a <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant continuous family of right inverses <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now using the product construction, for <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Floer</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=pr_{1}\cdots r_{l}q\in{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a fiberwise Banach manifold <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{\delta(\alpha)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{\delta(\alpha)}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding linearized Cauchy–Riemann operator <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\alpha,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\alpha,x}</annotation></semantics></math> at each <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\pi_{\alpha}^{-1}(\underline{\smash{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\pi_{\alpha}^{-1}(\underline{\smash{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. A chosen family of right inverses <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pr_{1}},\ldots,T_{r_{l}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pr_{1}},\ldots,T_{r_{l}q}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a family of right inverse <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\alpha,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\alpha,x}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now as we have another layer of thickening, and we would like to solve the corresponding equation for this “thicker” problem, we need to stabilize the right inverse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">First, notice that we have the natural inclusions</p>
<table id="S5.Ex221" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex221.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lx@xy@svg{\hbox{\raise 2.5pt\hbox{\kern 13.02301pt\hbox{\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\hbox{\vtop{\halign{\entry@#!@&amp;&amp;\entry@@#!@\cr&amp;\cr&amp;\crcr}}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern-12.88412pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 37.16191pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise-24.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 37.16191pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{E}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 51.46599pt\raise-24.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern-13.02301pt\raise-31.33331pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{B}}_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 37.02301pt\raise-31.33331pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 37.02301pt\raise-31.33331pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces}}}}\ignorespaces." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext> <svg style="vertical-align:-55.93985661408607px" height="66.09" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="18.02 55.94 109.22 66.09" width="109.22"> <g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 18.019939468529472 66.08696189744188) translate(0,3.46) translate(18.02,0)"> <g transform="translate(-17.83,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.98em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.36em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.43" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="27.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></span></span></foreignObject> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 17.827763602960253 -0.27673578005007266 L 17.827763602960253 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(51.42,0)"> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 17.827763602960253 -0.27673578005007266 L 51.420936779773335 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 51.420936779773335 -0.27673578005007266 L 51.420936779773335 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -12.583950998642244 L 0.27673578005007266 -12.583950998642244" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(0,-33.21)"> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -12.583950998642244" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088 L 0.27673578005007266 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(51.42,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.26em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.36em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.43" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="31.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></span></span></foreignObject> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 70.93675274794175 -12.583950998642244 L 71.49022430804189 -12.583950998642244" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(71.21,0) translate(0,-33.21)"> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 70.93675274794175 -33.208800332088 L 71.49022430804189 -12.583950998642244" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 70.93675274794175 -33.208800332088 L 71.49022430804189 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(-18.02,0) translate(0,-43.36) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.36em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.43" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="27.74"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></span></span></foreignObject> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 18.01996058211611 -43.63264139549389 L 18.01996058211611 -43.07916983539375" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(51.23,0) translate(0,-43.36)"> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 18.01996058211611 -43.63264139549389 L 51.228739800617475 -43.07916983539375" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 51.228739800617475 -43.63264139549389 L 51.228739800617475 -43.07916983539375" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(51.23,0) translate(0,-43.36) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.29em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.36em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.43" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="31.67"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub></span></span></foreignObject> </g> </g> </svg> </mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lx@xy@svg{\hbox{\raise 2.5pt\hbox{\kern 13.02301pt\hbox{\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\hbox{\vtop{\halign{\entry@#!@&amp;&amp;\entry@@#!@\cr&amp;\cr&amp;\crcr}}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern-12.88412pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 37.16191pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise-24.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 37.16191pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{E}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 51.46599pt\raise-24.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern-13.02301pt\raise-31.33331pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{B}}_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 37.02301pt\raise-31.33331pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 37.02301pt\raise-31.33331pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathcal{B}}_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces}}}}\ignorespaces.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">The differences are given by deformations/obstructions in the difference between the two bundles over <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha,\underline{\smash{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By looking at the thickened equation (<a href="#S5.E32" title="In Definition 5.45 (Multi-layered thickening). ‣ 5.3.3. Multi-layered thickening ‣ 5.3. Global chart construction II ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.32</span></a>), we can see that the linearization at <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{\alpha}\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{\alpha}\subset\partial^{\alpha}V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is block upper-triangular</p>
<table id="S5.Ex222" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex222.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{pq,x}=\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}D_{\alpha,x}&amp;*\\
0&amp;D_{pq,\alpha,x}\end{array}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>∗</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{pq,x}=\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}D_{\alpha,x}&amp;*\\
0&amp;D_{pq,\alpha,x}\end{array}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{pq,\alpha,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{pq,\alpha,x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjective linear Cauchy–Riemann operator. Hence we can extend <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\alpha,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\alpha,x}</annotation></semantics></math> to a block-upper triangular right inverse</p>
<table id="S5.Ex223" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex223.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq,x}=\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}T_{\alpha,x}&amp;*\\
0&amp;T_{pq,\alpha,x}\end{array}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>∗</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq,x}=\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}T_{\alpha,x}&amp;*\\
0&amp;T_{pq,\alpha,x}\end{array}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.4" class="ltx_p">In fact this only depends on the choice of the right inverse <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{pq,\alpha,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{pq,\alpha,x}</annotation></semantics></math> and we can make this choice continuously depending on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The last ingredient we need for applying the implicit function theorem is a choice of local charts in the Banach manifold and local frames of the Banach vector bundle. In fact there is a natural one coming from the Riemannian metric on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}\in B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fiberwise Banach manifold is a space of maps into the total space of a vector bundle <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{pq,\underline{\smash{x}}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> which is essentially a tensor bundle over <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the parallel transport using the Levi–Civita connection associated to the metric <math id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(\cdot,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,J)</annotation></semantics></math> induces local charts of the fiberwise Banach manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to say that so far the right inverse and the local charts/frames are chosen for points which are in normal position. If we go slightly away from the locus of normal position, the parallel transport along shortest geodesics automatically produce a family of local charts/frames as well as right inverses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.5.3. </span>Definition of the stabilization map</h4>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can describe the stabilization map. Let us temporarily denote by <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> the nonlinear Cauchy–Riemann operator (<a href="#S5.E32" title="In Definition 5.45 (Multi-layered thickening). ‣ 5.3.3. Multi-layered thickening ‣ 5.3. Global chart construction II ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.32</span></a>). First, apply the approximate solution map (<a href="#S5.E47" title="In 5.5.2. Analytical setup ‣ 5.5. Stabilization maps ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.47</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S5.Ex224" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex224.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{pq,\alpha}^{\rm app}:{\rm Stab}_{F_{pq,\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\to\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>app</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Stab</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{pq,\alpha}^{\rm app}:{\rm Stab}_{F_{pq,\alpha}}(V_{\alpha})\to\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}^{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(which is in fact only defined in a small disk bundle in <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{pq,\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{pq,\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>). Then there exists <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta_{pq,\alpha},h_{pq,\alpha})\in F_{pq,\alpha}|_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta_{pq,\alpha},h_{pq,\alpha})\in F_{pq,\alpha}|_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\eta_{pq,\alpha}\|+\|h_{pq,\alpha}\|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\eta_{pq,\alpha}\|+\|h_{pq,\alpha}\|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{pq}(\theta^{\rm app}_{pq,\alpha}(x,\eta_{pq,\alpha},h_{pq,\alpha}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>app</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{pq}(\theta^{\rm app}_{pq,\alpha}(x,\eta_{pq,\alpha},h_{pq,\alpha}))</annotation></semantics></math> is very small. Then using the local charts on the fiberwise Banach manifold induced from the Levi–Civita connection centered at <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding right inverse of the linear operator, one obtains an exact solution to <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{pq}(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{pq}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> which is sufficiently close to the approximate solution and its difference with the approximate solution is contained in the image of the right inverse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are several things to check. First, we need to show that the map is continuous. In fact, this is due to the continuity of the family of right inverses and approximate solutions and how Gromov topology on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined. Second, we show that the map is injective. This is a consequence of the implicit function theorem and the fact that (on each fiber over <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>), the approximate solution is transverse to the image of the right inverse we use. Third, we show that the map is onto an open subset. Indeed, from the construction we know that the stabilization map is onto an open subset on each fiber over <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. As the image also covers a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\delta(\alpha)}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\delta(\alpha)}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S5.SS5.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{\delta(\alpha)}B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{\delta(\alpha)}B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the stabilization map is onto an open subset. In fact, this is exactly the content of the gluing theorem presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Par16</a>, Appendix C]</cite>. Again, the reader could refer to Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm22" title="Proposition 6.22. ‣ 6.2.1. Topological submersions ‣ 6.2. Pre-smoothing operations and stable complex structures ‣ 6. Smoothing ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.22</span></a> to see how to implement the aforementioned theorem into our setting. Lastly, it satisfies the stratum-preserving property (<a href="#S5.E45" title="In item 2c ‣ item 2 ‣ Proposition 5.55. ‣ 5.5. Stabilization maps ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.45</span></a>) which can be seen from the fact that the right inverses we used are all block upper-triangular.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm61" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm61.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.61</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm61.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm61.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm61.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is natural to require the stabilization map to be compatible with compositions of chart embeddings. In fact to achieve this goal one needs to make a number of compatible choices, including showing that such compatible choices exist. The choices include a system of compatible inner products on the obstruction bundles, a system of stabilization maps, and a system of bundle identifications. The details are given in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C A proof sketch of Proposition ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6. </span>Weak K-chart lift</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the construction we have done. The following theorem is a more comprehensive version of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5.1.5. The main statement ‣ 5.1. Basic notions and the main theorem about global charts ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm62" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm62.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.62</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm62.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm62.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm62.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm62.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist the following objects.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I23" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I23.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I23.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I23.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I23.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A weak K-chart lift of </span><math id="S5.I23.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\rm Floer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>Floer</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\rm Floer}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I23.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (see Definition </span><a href="#S5.Thmthm9" title="Definition 5.9. ‣ 5.1.5. The main statement ‣ 5.1. Basic notions and the main theorem about global charts ‣ 5. Global Kuranishi charts on Floer moduli spaces ‣ Arnold Conjecture over Integers" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a><span id="S5.I23.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">), consisting of a collection of K-charts </span><math id="S5.I23.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{pq}=(G_{pq},V_{pq},E_{pq},S_{pq},\psi_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{pq}=(G_{pq},V_{pq},E_{pq},S_{pq},\psi_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I23.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and a collection of weak K-chart embeddings</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex225" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex225.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\iota}}_{prq}:K_{pr}\times K_{rq}\rightharpoonup\partial^{prq}K_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜾</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">⇀</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\iota}}_{prq}:K_{pr}\times K_{rq}\rightharpoonup\partial^{prq}K_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I23.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I23.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(which satisfy the required conditions).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I23.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I23.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I23.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I23.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A collection of </span><math id="S5.I23.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I23.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant stratified maps</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex226" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex226.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vbox{\lx@xy@svg{\hbox{\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\kern 16.15283pt\hbox{\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\hbox{\vtop{\halign{\entry@#!@&amp;&amp;\entry@@#!@\cr&amp;\cr&amp;\crcr}}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern-11.29712pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.55554pt\hbox{$\textstyle{V_{pq}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 17.50755pt\raise 5.49306pt\hbox{{}\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise-0.52084pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle{\pi_{pq}}$}}}\kern 3.0pt}}}}}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 40.15283pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise-23.17165pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 40.15283pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.55554pt\hbox{$\textstyle{B_{d_{pq}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 53.36392pt\raise-24.82835pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern-16.15283pt\raise-32.10612pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.55554pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathbb{A}}_{pq}^{\rm Floer}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 21.8646pt\raise-37.53667pt\hbox{{}\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise-2.43056pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle{\delta}$}}}\kern 3.0pt}}}}}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 40.44623pt\raise-32.10612pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 40.44623pt\raise-32.10612pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.55554pt\hbox{$\textstyle{{\mathbb{A}}_{d_{pq}}}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces}}}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext> <svg style="vertical-align:-59.453748610894905px" height="74.66" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="22.35 59.45 114.47 74.66" width="114.47"> <g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 22.350673905147364 74.65521428567567) translate(22.35,0)"> <g transform="translate(-15.63,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.66em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="22.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></span></span></foreignObject> </g> <path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 15.631823911201051 -0.27673578005007266 L 15.631823911201051 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path> <g transform="translate(24.23,0) translate(0,7.6) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-0.72)"> <foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="17.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em